{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:44:55.033818+00:00", "level": "INFO", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "agent_prompt", "agent": "pi", "prompt": "# DataSQRL Planning & Q&A\n\n## Request\n\n---\ntype: user-requirements\n---\n\n# Requirements: Loan Payments (personal loans)\n\n## Goal\n\nGive each personal-loan customer an API to look up their own loans: the payment summary of each\nloan and the payments of each loan. The customer signs in through the SSO, and the API shows only\nthe loans of the `customer_id` in their JWT.\n\n## Scope\n\n**In scope:**\n\n- A new DataSQRL project in `loan-payments/` that reads the finance data catalog\n  (`data-catalog/`, a git submodule at the repository root) and does not change it.\n- The table `LoanPayments`: one row per payment of a personal loan, with the customer id and the\n  loan purpose from the loan record.\n- The table `LoanPaymentSummary`: one row per personal loan, with payment counts and totals.\n- An API with the three lookups in [API surface](#api-surface), authorized by the JWT claim\n  `customer_id`.\n- Tests that use the test data of the catalog.\n- A `test` and a `prod` environment, with the same layout as the sibling project `lending/`\n  (`lending360-shared-package.json`, `lending360-test-package.json`, `lending360-prod-package.json`).\n\n**Out of scope:**\n\n- Auto loans and auto-loan payments. The catalog table `Loan_Payment` holds both, and this project\n  keeps only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'`.\n- Mortgages, credit cards and delinquency events.\n- Payment submission or any other write to the source systems. The API has no mutations.\n- Staff or admin access to the loans of other customers.\n- Changes to the data catalog, its schemas or its test data.\n- A web UI.\n\n## Functional requirements\n\n- **FR-1**: Read the personal loans from the catalog table `Personal_Loan` and the payments from\n  the catalog table `Loan_Payment` (dataset `lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans`).\n- **FR-2**: Keep the latest version of each personal loan (key `loan_id`) and of each payment\n  (key `payment_id`), by `source_updated_at`. Both sources are CDC streams.\n- **FR-3**: Keep only the payments of personal loans: `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` and a `loan_id`\n  that exists in `Personal_Loan`.\n- **FR-4**: `LoanPayments` has one row per `payment_id`. Each row has the payment fields listed in\n  [Transformations](#transformations-and-aggregations), plus `customer_id`\n  (= `Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id`) and `loan_purpose` from the loan record.\n- **FR-5**: `LoanPayments` keeps payments of every status (`APPLIED`, `PENDING`, `RETURNED`,\n  `REVERSED`) so that a customer sees their returned payments.\n- **FR-6**: `LoanPaymentSummary` has one row per personal loan, with `loan_id`, `customer_id`,\n  `loan_purpose` and these values:\n  - `applied_payment_count`: the number of payments with `payment_status = 'APPLIED'`.\n  - `returned_payment_count`: the number of payments with `payment_status = 'RETURNED'`.\n  - `total_paid_cents`: the sum of `payment_amount_cents`.\n  - `principal_paid_cents`: the sum of `principal_cents`.\n  - `interest_paid_cents`: the sum of `interest_cents`.\n  - `late_fees_paid_cents`: the sum of `late_fee_cents`.\n  - `last_payment_date`: the latest `applied_date`.\n- **FR-7**: The totals and `last_payment_date` in FR-6 use only the payments with\n  `payment_status = 'APPLIED'`. A `RETURNED`, `PENDING` or `REVERSED` payment adds nothing to them.\n- **FR-8**: When a payment changes status (for example `APPLIED` to `RETURNED`), the summary of its\n  loan uses only the latest status.\n- **FR-9**: Query `LoanPaymentSummary` by `loan_id`.\n- **FR-10**: Query the `LoanPayments` of a loan by `loan_id`.\n- **FR-11**: Query all loans of a customer, each with its summary, by `customer_id`.\n- **FR-12**: Every query returns only rows whose `customer_id` equals the JWT claim `customer_id`.\n  A request for a loan of another customer returns no rows.\n- **FR-13**: A request without a valid JWT, or with a JWT that has no `customer_id` claim, is\n  rejected.\n\n## Sources\n\nBoth sources are catalog tables that the project imports. The project manages no data itself.\n\n### `Personal_Loan` (catalog)\n\n- **Ownership**: external. The consumer lending system publishes it. The project only reads it.\n- **Catalog files**: schema `data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans.sqrl`\n  (`Personal_Loan_schema`), connectors `consumer_loans-test.sqrl` and `consumer_loans-prod.sqrl`.\n- **Transport**: test: file `testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl`. prod: Kafka topic\n  `consumer_loans.personal_loan` on `${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}`.\n- **Format**: JSON, ISO-8601 timestamps. No schema registry.\n- **Sample payload** (test data):\n\n  ```json\n  {\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-023\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-066\",\n   \"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-28229\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"MAJOR_PURCHASE\",\n   \"original_amount_cents\":1740000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1566,\n   \"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\n   \"monthly_payment_cents\":84913,\"funding_date\":\"2020-09-11\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-10-11\",\n   \"maturity_date\":\"2022-09-11\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-09-04\",\n   \"origination_fee_cents\":34800,\"credit_score_at_origination\":644,\"dti_at_origination\":24.62,\n   \"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\n   \"origination_channel\":\"BRANCH\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\n   \"source_updated_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:40:57Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:44:22Z\"}\n  ```\n\n  Nullable fields used by this project: `loan_purpose`. `loan_id` and\n  `primary_borrower_customer_id` are `NOT NULL`.\n- **Volume and rate**: test data: 170 loans, 142 customers. Production volume is an open question.\n- **Delivery guarantee**: at-least-once (Kafka, `kafka-safe` connector). Replay from the earliest\n  offset is possible.\n- **Duplicates and ordering**: a CDC stream. Several versions of a `loan_id` can arrive. The latest\n  `source_updated_at` wins.\n- **Updates**: CDC updates to prior state (for example `ACTIVE` to `PAID_OFF`).\n\n### `Loan_Payment` (catalog)\n\n- **Ownership**: external, the same consumer lending system.\n- **Catalog files**: schema `Loan_Payment_schema` in the same `consumer_loans.sqrl`, with the same\n  connector files.\n- **Transport**: test: file `testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl`. prod: Kafka topic\n  `consumer_loans.loan_payment`.\n- **Format**: JSON, ISO-8601 timestamps.\n- **Sample payload** (test data, a returned payment):\n\n  ```json\n  {\"payment_id\":\"LPMT-046\",\"loan_id\":\"PL-115\",\"loan_type\":\"PERSONAL\",\"payment_amount_cents\":57760,\n   \"principal_cents\":37108,\"interest_cents\":20652,\"late_fee_cents\":0,\"other_fees_cents\":0,\n   \"payment_type\":\"REGULAR\",\"payment_status\":\"RETURNED\",\"due_date\":\"2025-10-08\",\n   \"received_date\":\"2025-10-05\",\"applied_date\":\"2025-10-05\",\"payment_method\":\"ACH\",\n   \"principal_balance_after_cents\":1632892,\"days_past_due_at_payment\":0,\n   \"confirmation_number\":\"ACH-20251005-00046\",\"return_reason_code\":\"R01\",\n   \"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2025-10-05T14:36:25Z\",\n   \"ingested_at\":\"2025-10-05T14:39:42Z\"}\n  ```\n\n  Nullable fields: `principal_cents`, `interest_cents`, `late_fee_cents`, `other_fees_cents`,\n  `received_date`, `applied_date`, `payment_method`, `principal_balance_after_cents`,\n  `days_past_due_at_payment`, `confirmation_number`, `return_reason_code`.\n- **Volume and rate**: test data: 1,942 payments. 1,010 are personal (1,001 `APPLIED`,\n  9 `RETURNED`). The test data has no `PENDING` or `REVERSED` payment.\n- **Delivery guarantee**: at-least-once.\n- **Duplicates and ordering**: a CDC stream. Deduplicate on `payment_id`, the latest\n  `source_updated_at` wins.\n- **Updates**: CDC. A payment can change status after it arrives.\n\n## Time semantics\n\n- **Event-time field**: `ingested_at` (`TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3)`, UTC). The catalog connectors set the\n  watermark on it: 10 seconds in test, 30 seconds in prod. The project uses these watermarks as\n  they are.\n- **Version order**: `source_updated_at` picks the latest version of a loan or a payment.\n- **Lateness**: a payment that arrives late still counts. The summary is not windowed, so a late\n  payment updates the summary of its loan.\n- **Processing time**: not used.\n\n## Entities and keys\n\n| Entity | Key | Source | Relations |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Personal loan | `loan_id` | catalog `Personal_Loan` | belongs to one customer (`primary_borrower_customer_id`) |\n| Payment | `payment_id` | catalog `Loan_Payment` | belongs to one loan (`loan_id`) |\n| Customer | `customer_id` | the JWT claim, and `Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id` | has zero or more personal loans |\n\nThe `customer_id` of a payment comes from its loan, not from the payment record.\n\n## Transformations and aggregations\n\n1. **Latest loans**: the latest version of each `Personal_Loan` by `source_updated_at`, key\n   `loan_id`. All loan statuses stay (`ACTIVE`, `PAID_OFF`, `CHARGED_OFF`, `SETTLED`).\n2. **Latest payments**: the latest version of each `Loan_Payment` by `source_updated_at`, key\n   `payment_id`, filtered to `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'`.\n3. **`LoanPayments`**: latest payments joined to latest loans on `loan_id` (inner join). Columns:\n   `payment_id`, `loan_id`, `customer_id`, `loan_purpose`, `payment_amount_cents`,\n   `principal_cents`, `interest_cents`, `late_fee_cents`, `other_fees_cents`, `payment_type`,\n   `payment_status`, `due_date`, `received_date`, `applied_date`, `payment_method`,\n   `principal_balance_after_cents`, `days_past_due_at_payment`, `confirmation_number`,\n   `return_reason_code`. Default order: `due_date` descending, then `payment_id`.\n4. **`LoanPaymentSummary`**: group `LoanPayments` by `loan_id`, with the values of FR-6 and the\n   filter rule of FR-7. Window: none (unbounded, updated on each change). A loan without payments\n   gets a row with counts and sums of 0 and a null `last_payment_date` (see Open Questions).\n\nAmounts stay in cents (`BIGINT`), the same unit as the catalog.\n\n## Results\n\n### `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`\n\n- **Kind**: API (served from the database of the project).\n- **Consumers**: personal-loan customers, through the client app of the bank.\n- **Authorization**: authorized. The JWT from the SSO carries the claim `customer_id`. Every query\n  filters on `customer_id = <claim customer_id>`. No other claim gives access.\n\n## API surface\n\n- **Queries** (all filtered by the JWT claim `customer_id`, see FR-12):\n  - `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)`: the summary of one loan. Returns 0 or 1 row.\n  - `LoanPayments(loan_id)`: the payments of one loan, newest due date first, paged\n    (`limit`, `offset`).\n  - Loans of a customer: all personal loans of the signed-in customer, each with its summary.\n    The `customer_id` comes from the JWT claim, so the caller cannot ask for another customer.\n- **Mutations**: none.\n- **Subscriptions**: none.\n- **Protocols**: GraphQL, REST and MCP, the same as the sibling project `lending/`.\n- **Access**: every query needs a valid JWT with the claim `customer_id`.\n\n## Test data and acceptance criteria\n\n- **Test data**: the catalog test data in\n  `data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/`\n  (`consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl`, `consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl`), read through the\n  catalog test connectors. The project adds no source data of its own.\n- **Edge cases in that data**:\n  - Auto-loan payments (932 rows) that the filter must remove.\n  - 9 `RETURNED` personal payments, on loans `PL-023`, `PL-061`, `PL-062`, `PL-068`, `PL-071`,\n    `PL-079`, `PL-108`, `PL-115`, `PL-121`.\n  - 26 applied personal payments with `late_fee_cents > 0`.\n  - 35 personal loans without payments (for example `PL-011`, `PL-014`).\n  - 23 customers with more than one personal loan (for example `CUST-060` with `PL-006`,\n    `PL-007`, `PL-022`, `PL-033`).\n  - Loans in every status (`ACTIVE` 114, `PAID_OFF` 51, `CHARGED_OFF` 4, `SETTLED` 1).\n- **Acceptance criteria**:\n  - *(FR-1, FR-3)* `LoanPayments` has 1,010 rows. No row has a `loan_id` that starts with `AL-`.\n  - *(FR-2)* `LoanPayments` has one row per `payment_id`, and `LoanPaymentSummary` has one row\n    per `loan_id`.\n  - *(FR-4)* Each `LoanPayments` row has the `customer_id` and `loan_purpose` of its loan in\n    `Personal_Loan`.\n  - *(FR-5)* `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` includes payment `LPMT-046` with\n    `payment_status = \"RETURNED\"` and `return_reason_code = \"R01\"`.\n  - *(FR-6, FR-7)* For `PL-115`, `returned_payment_count = 1`, and `total_paid_cents` equals the\n    sum of `payment_amount_cents` of its `APPLIED` payments only (it excludes the 57,760 of\n    `LPMT-046`). The planner computes the expected values from the test data.\n  - *(FR-6)* For a loan with applied late fees, `late_fees_paid_cents` equals the sum of their\n    `late_fee_cents`.\n  - *(FR-6)* For `PL-011` (no payments), the summary row has counts and sums of 0 and a null\n    `last_payment_date`.\n  - *(FR-8)* Not testable with the catalog test data, because no payment changes status in it.\n    The plan says how it covers this rule.\n  - *(FR-9, FR-12)* With a JWT for `CUST-439` (the customer of `PL-115`), `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: \"PL-115\")`\n    returns 1 row. With a JWT for `CUST-060`, the same query returns 0 rows.\n  - *(FR-10, FR-12)* With a JWT for `CUST-060`, `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` returns 0 rows.\n  - *(FR-11)* With a JWT for `CUST-060`, the customer query returns the 4 loans `PL-006`, `PL-007`,\n    `PL-022`, `PL-033`, each with its summary.\n  - *(FR-13)* A request without a JWT gets an authorization error.\n  - Snapshot tests store the API responses above under the test folder of the project.\n\n## Non-functional requirements\n\n- **Freshness**: a new or changed payment shows in `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary` within\n  1 minute of its arrival in Kafka (see Open Questions).\n- **API latency SLA**: p99 < 200 ms for each query, at up to 50 requests/second (see Open Questions).\n- **Workload**: unknown in production (see Open Questions). The test data has 170 loans and\n  1,010 personal payments.\n- **Budget**: none stated (see Open Questions).\n- **Reliability**: no data loss. After a failure, the pipeline restarts from its last checkpoint\n  and replays from Kafka.\n- **Retention**: the API keeps all payments of all personal loans, with no expiry.\n- **PII and sensitive fields**: `customer_id` is tagged `@pii`. The amounts are `@npi`. The API\n  shows them only to the customer that owns the loan (FR-12). The API leaves out `loan_number`\n  (`@npi @mask`), the account ids, the credit score and the DTI ratio.\n- **Compliance**: GLBA, GDPR, CCPA, the same as the catalog dataset. No new storage outside the\n  database of the project.\n- **Availability**: no target stated. The default deployment of DataSQRL Cloud applies.\n\n## Hard constraints\n\n- Use the catalog tables `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` from `data-catalog/` through\n  `script.include`, the same way as `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`. Do not copy or\n  re-declare them. Reason: the user asked to use the finance data catalog.\n- Use the catalog test data for the tests. Reason: the user asked for it.\n- Use the table names `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`. Reason: the user named them.\n- Authorize with the JWT claim `customer_id` from the SSO. Reason: the user stated it.\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- **Freshness**: how fast must a payment show in the API? Recommended default: *within 1 minute,\n  with the streaming Flink pipeline of the sibling `lending/`*. Impact if wrong: high.\n- **JWT validation**: which signing algorithm, issuer, audience and key does the SSO use?\n  Recommended default: *HS256 with a fixed test secret in `test`, and the key from an environment\n  variable (for example `${JWT_SECRET}`) in `prod`*. Impact if wrong: high.\n- **Production workload**: how many loans and payments per day, and how many API requests per\n  second? Recommended default: *up to 1 million personal loans, 50,000 payments per day, and\n  50 requests/second peak*. Impact if wrong: medium.\n- **Last payment date**: is it the latest `applied_date` of an applied payment, or the latest\n  `received_date`? Recommended default: *the latest `applied_date` of an `APPLIED` payment*.\n  Impact if wrong: low.\n- **Total amount paid**: is it `payment_amount_cents` (the full payment, which includes fees)?\n  Recommended default: *yes, the sum of `payment_amount_cents` of applied payments*. Impact if\n  wrong: low.\n- **Loans without payments**: does `LoanPaymentSummary` show a row for a loan with no payments?\n  Recommended default: *yes, with counts and sums of 0 and a null `last_payment_date`*. Impact if\n  wrong: medium.\n- **Loan status**: does the API show loans in every status, or only `ACTIVE` loans? Recommended\n  default: *every status*. Impact if wrong: medium.\n- **Loan details in the customer query**: does the customer query show loan fields other than the\n  summary (for example `loan_status`, `current_balance_cents`)? Recommended default: *add\n  `loan_status`, `original_amount_cents`, `current_balance_cents`, `monthly_payment_cents` and\n  `next_payment_due_date`*. Impact if wrong: low.\n- **API protocols**: GraphQL, REST and MCP, or GraphQL only? Recommended default: *all three, the\n  same as `lending/`*. Impact if wrong: low.\n- **Budget**: what may the pipeline cost per month? Recommended default: *the smallest DataSQRL\n  Cloud deployment size*. Impact if wrong: low.\n\nRequirements file: `adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`\n\n## Classify the Request\n\nBased on the workspace you have read and the request above, determine which applies:\n\n**QUESTION** - the user is asking a factual or conceptual question about DataSQRL, SQRL syntax, or this project. There is no intent to implement or change anything.\n\n**IMPLEMENTATION REQUEST** \u2014 the user wants to build, extend, or change a DataSQRL project. Even if the request is vague or high-level, if the intent is to produce working code, treat it as an implementation request.\n\nThese are mutually exclusive. Choose one and follow only that branch below.\n\n---\n\n## Branch A: QUESTION\n\nAnswer the question concisely and accurately. Use the workspace context and skill documentation you have read to inform your answer.\nDo NOT write any files. Do NOT generate an ADR. Your final response IS the answer.\n\n---\n\n## Branch B: IMPLEMENTATION REQUEST\n\nWork through the sections below in order: survey the repository, classify the request along two axes, write the plan file, and finish with the single-line response.\n\nThe plan is a short, high-level decomposition of the work into steps that a developer can review in a few minutes. Downstream implementation agent is going to read the generated plan and puts the details as it builds. Specific details like table names, record-level test values are the decisions of the downstream implementation agent.\n\n### 1. Survey the Repository\n\nA project can live inside a repository that holds **other SQRL projects and shared modules** (most often a data catalog). Survey them before classifying the request.\n\nHigh level repo contents (paths, READMEs, SQRL file lists, package summaries) are placed in `repo-inventory.md`. **Read that file first**, then deepen selectively:\n\n1. **Data catalogs and shared modules first.** They define the source tables everything else builds on. Read the module's `README.md` if present, then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`. Note the table names, schemas and the `` template variables it expects.\n2. **Then the other projects.** with the same order. Read `README.md`, then `.sqrl`, then `package.json`. If those projects use shared module or shared data catalog, look at how they declare `script.include` in package file and how they import the catalog.\n3. **Understand upstream guarantees and existing monitoring.** For the sources this job uses, read the assertions to learn what the data guarantees. Identify raw and cleaned tables (Raw tables contain records before the relevant upstream cleanup; cleaned tables expose the result with documented guarantees.), explicitly documented exceptions to those guarantees, and existing monitoring relevant to this job. Prefer the cleaned catalog tables unless this job is responsible for cleaning or enriching raw data.\n\nHard rules while surveying:\n\n- Everything outside the project is mounted **READ-ONLY**. Never create, edit or delete anything there, and never copy their files into the current project. \n- A shared module or a sibling SQRL project can be consumed by declaring it under `script.include` in package file (namespace \u2192 `package` path to any top-level package file of the module; that file's folder and all its subfolders are loaded) and importing it by that namespace in sqrl files.\n- **Never read another project's `build/` directory.**\n- `.sqrl` and `package.json` are enough to understand a sibling SQRL project. Do not read their test data if it is not compulsory.\n\nUse what you find, during exploration, to choose/write the right steps: which shared module or catalog the project reuses instead of rebuilding, which existing files a step extends, and which existing monitoring already covers a source.\n\n### 2. Classify the Request Along Two Axes\n\nClassify the request along **two independent axes**: the **artifact type** (`scenario`) and the **task type**. These are orthogonal, determine each separately. Record both in the plan header; the reasoning behind them stays in your analysis.\n\n#### Axis 1 \u2014 Artifact type (`scenario`)\n\nThis is recorded in the ADR frontmatter as `scenario` and **drives which verifiers run** during implementation. There are exactly two values:\n\n- **`pipeline`** \u2014 a DataSQRL pipeline: ingests from sources, processes/aggregates data, and exposes an API (GraphQL/REST/MCP). Files are `.sqrl` scripts plus `*-package.json` files that configure sqrl pipeline.\n- **`catalog`** \u2014 a standalone data catalog: a shared repository of schema definitions (`{dataset}.sqrl` with `_schema` tables), per-environment connector files (`{dataset}-<env>.sqrl`), ontologies, and test data, used across multiple pipelines. Focus is schema documentation, governance, and relationship modelling, **not** pipeline transformation logic or a GraphQL API.\n\n#### Axis 2 \u2014 Task type\n\nThis shapes the plan (it selects which questions to answer below) but is **not** parsed by the system. Record it in the ADR body as `**Task**:` (not in the frontmatter). Pick the task that best matches the artifact type you chose in Axis 1.\n\nThe questions below guide your analysis. Their answers shape the checklist steps and the few decisions worth recording; they are not written into the plan one by one.\n\n##### For `scenario: pipeline`\n\n**`new-project`** \u2014 No `.sqrl` files exist; building a pipeline from scratch.\n- What are the data sources (Kafka topics, databases, files)?\n- What processing and aggregations are required?\n- What is the desired API(s) (GraphQL queries, REST, MCP tools)?\n- Which engines are needed (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg, Vert.x, Snowflake, SparkSQL, Redshift, Trino)?\n- Which template in `/opt/datasqrl-examples/` is the closest starting point?\n- Are any custom Java functions (UDFs) required for processing logic?\n\n**`extend-project`** \u2014 `.sqrl` and `*-package.json` files already exist; adding or modifying\nfunctionality in an existing pipeline.\n- What tables, functions, or relationships need to be added or changed?\n- What is the impact on existing downstream tables, API, and tests?\n- Which existing snapshots need to be regenerated?\n- Are there naming or ordering conflicts with existing definitions?\n- Do any connectors need to be added or reconfigured?\n- Are any custom Java functions (UDFs) required for processing logic?\n\n**`connector-setup`** \u2014 Configuring data sources, sinks, or engine settings \u2014 not writing\npipeline logic.\n- Which connector type (Kafka, Iceberg, Postgres, file, etc.)?\n- What environment-specific configuration is required (dev vs. test vs. prod)?\n- Are there security or authentication settings needed?\n- Does the connector configuration need to be split across environment files?\n- Do existing SQRL scripts need to be updated to use the new connector?\n\n**`architecture-design`** \u2014 A domain problem with no technical architecture decided yet; requires\nfull design before any code is written.\n- What are the latency and throughput requirements?\n- Which engines are appropriate for each stage?\n- Where are the stateful operations and how can state growth be bounded?\n- What is the GraphQL API schema shape?\n- Are there non-incremental aggregations, or complete-snapshot file sources, that require special\n  handling (e.g. per-snapshot aggregation via a `SESSION` window to avoid double-counting;\n  bounded state)?\n- Are custom UDFs or a UI layer required?\n\n##### For `scenario: catalog`\n\n**`new-catalog`** \u2014 Creating a standalone data catalog from scratch.\n- What organizational hierarchy applies \u2014 single-team flat structure or line-of-business \u2192 team\n  hierarchy?\n- What datasets are in scope: which are source datasets (raw ingestion from external systems) vs\n  derived datasets (enriched or conformed views built on top of sources)?\n- What governance framework is required: classification levels, tagging conventions (`@pii`,\n  `@gdpr`, `@encrypt`), data ownership?\n- What environments are needed \u2014 test (filesystem connector) and/or prod (Kafka/Iceberg/other)?\n- What ontology relationships need to be defined across datasets (and across organizational\n  units)?\n- What test data is available or needs to be created for each dataset?\n- What data quality rules and constraints apply to each dataset \u2014 e.g. valid enum values,\n  mandatory fields, referential integrity, no self-references \u2014 that should be enforced as\n  `/*+test(no_rows) */` assertions in unit ontology files?\n\n**`extend-catalog`** \u2014 Adding, modifying, or reorganizing datasets, ontologies, or test data in an existing catalog.\n- Which existing line-of-business / team folder do the new or changed datasets map to?\n- What layout does the catalog use today, and does the request ask to change it? A specific layout restructure request is the only reason to move existing files. When it does ask, name the target layout, list every file that moves from its current path to its new path, and name the imports and test data paths that change with them.\n- What is the impact on existing ontology relationships and data quality assertions?\n- Do new datasets follow the catalog's established governance and tagging conventions?\n- Which existing test snapshots need to be regenerated?\n- Are there naming conflicts with existing datasets or schema tables?\n\n### 3. Self-Containment Rule\n\nDownstreaming implementation agent is going to receive the plan, and it runs inside the project, so it can read project files directly. In the plan, every specification, payload, expected value, and requirement text required to build the project is either present in the plan file or in a project file this plan references by path.\n\n- **Reference the requirements file.** The request above is saved in the project at the given path. Payloads, schemas, API contracts, test records and acceptance criteria stay in that file, and the implementation agent reads them there.\n- **Reference other project files by path.** An API schema or operations file, an existing `.sqrl` script, a `*-package.json` or a data file is referenced by its path when needed. Every file the plan references must be in the project.\n- **Point steps at requirements by their own labels.** Cite a requirement by the identifier or heading it has in the requirements source (`FR-3`, `### Source payloads`), so the implementation agent can look it up.\n\n### 4. Plan Section Content Rule\n\nPlan file sections are short prose. They say **what** must be achieved and **why**, and leave **how** to downstream implementation agent.\n\nLeave to the next stage (implementation agent): the specific details that are not written in the requirements but are going to be done like names of tables, columns, function scripts.\n\n\n### 5. Write the Plan File\n\nWrite a concise, actionable implementation plan to: `/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md`\nThe path already contains a unique timestamp. Create the plan file skeleton and then write it part by part. Do NOT modify any other file in the `adr/` directory.\n\n#### Build the file section by section\n\nBuild the plan in two stages: one Write for the skeleton, then one Edit per section. Each Edit lands its section permanently the moment it returns, so an interrupted run still leaves the finished sections on disk.\n\n1. **Skeleton.** One `Write`: frontmatter, title, the `**Scenario**` / `**Task**` / `**Date**` / `**Status**` lines, the `---` rule, and every `## ` heading in *ADR Format* order, each followed by `[PENDING: <section name>]`. Naming the section keeps every marker unique, so a later `Edit` matches exactly one place.\n2. **Fill.** One `Edit` per section, top to bottom, replacing that section's marker. Keep each `Edit` under about 100 lines of new text, and split a longer section across consecutive `Edit`s. One long `Edit` can hit the output limit and be lost causing waste of time, so make several consecutive `Edit`s in those cases.\n3. **Grow the file only through `Edit`.** A second `Write` to `/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md` replaces the whole file and rebuilds the problem this avoids.\n4. **Verify.** Before responding, re-read `/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md` and confirm no `[PENDING:` marker survives. A leftover marker means that section was never written: fill it now.\n\n#### Domain Identification\n\nIdentify which domain(s) apply based on the requirements and workspace content. \nMultiple domains may apply. List all that match. Use the signals below:\n\n| Domain | Apply when you see |\n|--------|--------------------|\n| `finance` | transactions, payments, portfolio, trading, balances, financial instruments |\n| `fraud` | fraud detection, anomaly detection, risk scoring, suspicious activity |\n| `healthcare` | medical records, patient data, clinical events, diagnoses, prescriptions |\n| `ecommerce` | orders, products, shopping cart, inventory, shipments, storefronts |\n| `iot` | sensor data, device telemetry, machine readings, time-series streams |\n| `analytics` | dashboards, business intelligence, KPIs, reporting, aggregated metrics |\n\nIf the requirements span multiple domains (e.g. fraud detection in e-commerce), list all that apply. If none match clearly, use an empty list.\n\n#### Data Quality & Observability Items\n\nAssess data quality and observability for this particular job, even when the user does not ask for them. Focus on problems most likely to affect its results, consumers, or downstream processing. Record concise Build, Skip, and Reuse decisions; do not enumerate every hypothetical check.\n\n1. **Trust the data catalog.** Treat catalog assertions as upstream guarantees, not a list of tests to repeat in pipelines. Only treat an assertion as unenforced when the catalog or source explicitly documents that it is not enforced on source data and must be evaluated downstream. The fact that an assertion runs in tests does not establish that exception.\n2. **Only add source data quality check when the following conditions hold:** invalid data would break this job's processing or intended result, and such data can reach the job because the relevant upstream guarantee is absent or explicitly unenforced. Prefer cleaned catalog tables. A job responsible for cleaning or enriching raw records may read the raw table and capture invalid records or count them when retaining records would be too expensive.\n3. **Focus on failures that matter to this job.** Inspect joins, filters, and derived results for credible failures that would otherwise go unnoticed. For example, capture a transaction with no matching customer at event time only when that case is possible and silently losing the transaction harms this job's result.\n4. **Only add observability for critical blind spots.** Do not require hourly counts for every source or reproduce Flink metrics. For example, counts by department can be justified when departments send independently and one department stopping would harm the result while aggregate metrics hide it.\n5. **State each Build decision as a risk, not a table.** Say what would goes wrong, where in the pipeline it shows up, who acts on it, and whether it needs captured records (data quality) or counts (observability).\n\n#### ADR Format\n\nEvery implementation plan follows this structure, with the sections in this order:\n\n```\n## Requirements Source\n## Implementation Checklist\n## Data Quality & Observability\n## Technical Decisions\n## Assumptions\n```\n\nThe block below describe each section's content as copied material.\n```markdown\n---\ndomains:\n  - <domain>  # identify and list the domain(s) based on the requirements and workspace content. Use [] if none apply\nscenario: <pipeline | catalog>   # Axis 1 \u2014 artifact type; drives verification\n---\n\n# Implementation Plan: <short descriptive title>\n\n**Scenario**: <same value as the `scenario` field in the frontmatter above: pipeline | catalog>\n**Task**: <Axis 2 task type \u2014 for pipeline: new-project | extend-project | connector-setup | architecture-design; for catalog: new-catalog | extend-catalog>\n**Date**: <UTC datetime, e.g. 2026-06-08T14:30:00Z>\n**Status**: DRAFT\n\n<One or two sentences: what is being built and what it reuses.>\n\n---\n\n## Requirements Source\n<The path of the requirements file (e.g. `adr/requirements_20260608-120000.md`), plus any other project file that carries part of the specification (an API schema, a sample data file).>\n\n## Implementation Checklist\n\n<Each step is a markdown checkbox that keeps its sequential number (numbers are referenced by `[deps: ...]`). Write EVERY box unchecked (`- [ ]`) with enumaration.>\n\nExample:\n- [ ] 1. Initialize project from nearest template [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- [ ] 2. Connect the order events source (see `### Sources` in the requirements) [deps: 1] \u2192 skill: /manage-connector\n- [ ] 3. Build the event pipeline: ingest the event mutation, and compute the hourly per-namespace rollups served by the query operations in `api/operations.graphql`. [deps: 1, 2] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n\n...\n\n## Data Quality & Observability\n<Three short lists in plain prose, without code. Empty lists are valid; say so when nothing new is needed.\n\n- **Build** \u2014 each critical risk: what would go wrong, where it shows up, why upstream guarantees and existing coverage miss it, and who acts on it.\n- **Skip** \u2014 material decisions to omit monitoring, with the reason (the catalog guarantees it, existing metrics suffice, nobody would act).\n- **Reuse** \u2014 existing monitoring in the catalog or a sibling project that covers a need, by path.>\n\n## Technical Decisions\n<A few bullets, one or two sentences each: the architectural choices the requirements leave open (engine selection, ingestion or watermark strategy, snapshot-aggregation strategy, how the API is exposed, catalog layout), each with the constraint that determined it. Example: \"hourly rollups use a cumulative window, which emits the open hour within the 30 s freshness target; a tumbling window emits only at the hour boundary\">\n\n## Assumptions\n<Every unknown the planning agent encountered, along with the concrete default it chose. Make a decision and state it here, because the implementation agent proceeds from these assumptions and a question left open stalls its progress. Format each assumption as:\n\"**<topic>**: Assumed <decision>. Rationale: <one sentence why this default is reasonable.> [impact: high|medium|low]\"\n\nThe `[impact: ...]` tag rates how much the implementation would change if the assumption turned out wrong:\n- **high** \u2014 a wrong guess forces material rework: schema shape, primary keys, source/connector type, connector or import paths (e.g. `data_catalog.<lob>.<dataset>.<table>`), data semantics, or scope inclusion/exclusion.\n- **medium** \u2014 a localized change (a single table or one endpoint).\n- **low** \u2014 trivially adjustable later\n\nMark only the genuinely load-bearing assumptions `high`>\n```\n\n### 6. Checklist Rules\n\nThe implementation checklist is the most important section of the plan. It also serves as the\nimplementation agent's durable progress tracker: each step is a markdown checkbox the\nimplementation agent marks off as it goes, and re-reads to resume after a context compaction.\n\n1. **Every step is an unchecked checkbox** that starts with `- [ ] ` before its number:\n   `- [ ] 1. ...`, `- [ ] 2. ...`. Write the 4-state legend line (shown in the ADR Format\n   template) directly under the `## Implementation Checklist` heading so the plan file is\n   self-describing. Leave every box unchecked and never pre-mark a box `- [*]`, `- [x]`, or\n   `- [~]`. Those states are filled in later, only by the implementation agent as it works:\n   it marks a box `- [*]` when it starts a step and `- [x]` once that step compiles/passes.\n2. **Number every step** sequentially starting from 1, immediately after the checkbox. The\n   number is load-bearing \u2014 `[deps: ...]` references it \u2014 so keep the numbering contiguous.\n3. **Dependency annotation** is required on every step:\n   - `[deps: none]` \u2014 no dependencies, can run immediately\n   - `[deps: 1, 3]` \u2014 depends on steps 1 and 3 completing first\n4. **Condition annotation** for steps that only execute under certain circumstances:\n   - `[cond: <condition>]` \u2014 the step runs only when the stated condition is true\n   - Two kinds of conditions:\n     - **Reactive**: triggered by something going wrong during execution \u2014\n       e.g., `[cond: tests fail after step 5]`\n     - **Requirement-dependent**: triggered by a feature need the planner could not fully\n       determine \u2014 e.g., `[cond: custom Java processing logic required]`\n   - Omit `[cond]` entirely for unconditional steps (they always run)\n5. **Skill annotation** where a skill directly applies:\n   - Append skills as `\u2192 skill: /skill-name-1, /skill-name-2` after all other annotations\n   - Omit for steps that are general actions (reading, cleanup, git operations)\n6. **Steps that share the same dependencies and do not depend on each other** should be listed\n   as separate top-level checkbox lines (not a nested sub-list). They are candidates for parallel execution and are marked independently, in any order.\n7. **Order steps** so that all dependencies of a step appear before that step.\n8. **Each step is one or two sentences of outcome.** State what the step achieves, cite the requirement(s) it serves, name the existing files or modules it builds on, and append the `\u2192 skill:` that supplies the syntax.\n9. **Create/Update a test-runner step.** Every pipeline plan carries a step that creates or\n   updates `run-tests.sh` which is the entry point for the project's tests, and runs it.\n   (`\u2192 skill: /test-sqrl`).\n10. **Include a shared-base-config step.** A plan that creates a new project or catalog carries\n    a checklist step to write the `<project>-shared-package.json` base config plus thin\n    `<project>-<env>-package.json` overlays (`-test`, `-prod`, `-local`, `-dev`) (part of\n    `/configure-sqrl` and `/build-catalog`).\n    - **Give each pipeline project and sub-project a `-test`, a `-dev` and a `-prod` overlay.**\n      Leave out an environment only when the requirements say that the environment is not\n      needed, and record that in `## Assumptions`.\n    - **The `-dev` overlay** uses sources and sinks that run on a local machine, so the pipeline\n      compiles and runs without production credentials. A `-prod` config often needs credentials\n      or services that the container does not have.\n    - **Use `-dev` as the name of the local environment.** Add a `-local` overlay only when the\n      requirements ask for it. Two names for one environment make the implementation guess.\n11. **Add data quality and observability after building the pipeline.** Every pipeline plan must include one checklist step that covers the work listed in `## Data Quality & Observability`. Place this step after all tasks that build or change the pipeline, including sources, transformations, outputs, and API definitions. Use `[deps: ...]` to make it wait until those tasks are complete. In this step, inspect the completed pipeline and build the needed data quality tables and observability statistics tables. Place final testing and documentation after this step so they include the monitoring. If nothing new is needed, state that in the step and explain why.\n12. **Assess whether a UI is required.** Dashboards, charts or other user-facing views in the requirements call for UI steps even without an explicit UI request. When a UI is required, add steps annotated with `\u2192 skill: /implement-ui` for the required views and interactions and their setup documentation.\n\n### 7. Data-Catalog File Format (CRITICAL)\n\nWhen writing a checklist for `scenario: catalog`, **all catalog files use `.sqrl` extension**.\nDataSQRL data catalogs are NOT YAML/JSON/CSV catalogs \u2014 they are DataSQRL scripts.\n\n**Never use**: `dataset.yaml`, `dataset.json`, `schema.yaml`, `catalog/*.yaml`, `.csv` test data.\n**Always use**: `.sqrl` for all scripts, `.jsonl` for all test data, `.json` for package files.\n\n**See the file layout from the `/build-catalog` skill and choosing the layout.** Layout selection part of the `/build-catalog` skill gives the default layout for a new catalog, the rule that an existing catalog keeps its own unless specified, and the rule that a reorganization request makes the default layout the target if desired organization is not expressed. Read the catalog before writing the plan, and move existing files only when the user asks for a reorganization.\n\n**State the governing layout in `## Technical Decisions`** and give every checklist step a full file path that matches it.\n\nAlways inspect `/opt/datasqrl-examples/finance-catalog/` as the canonical reference before writing\nany catalog checklist steps.\n\nFor `scenario: catalog`, `/build-catalog` is the primary skill, and the compile/test checklist\nstep references `ontology-test-package.json`, never a pipeline package.\n\n### 8. Skill-to-Task Guidance\n\nUse this table as a starting point, and include a skill in the plan only when a concrete step requires it \u2014 never speculatively. Read the skill in full before you assign a skill to a checklist item, so the step's text matches what that skill actually requires.\n\n| Skill | new-project | extend-project | connector-setup | catalog | architecture-design |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `/build-catalog` | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2713 always | \u2014 |\n| `/init-sqrl` | \u2713 always | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2713 if new project |\n| `/implement-sqrl` | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 if scripts need updating | \u2014 | \u2713 always |\n| `/configure-sqrl` | \u2713 always | \u2713 if config changes | \u2713 always | \u2014 | \u2713 always |\n| `/design-api` | \u2713 always | \u2713 if API changes | \u2713 if API affected | \u2014 | \u2713 always |\n| `/from-api` | \u2713 if API spec given | \u2713 if API spec given | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2713 if API spec given |\n| `/manage-connector` | \u2713 always | \u2713 if connectors change | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always |\n| `/data-observation` | \u2713 if data files provided | \u2713 if new data files | \u2713 if source data provided | \u2713 if test data from files | \u2713 if data files provided |\n| `/handle-large-data-files` | \u2713 if large data files | \u2713 if large data files | \u2713 if large data files | \u2713 if large data files | \u2713 if large data files |\n| `/implement-udf` | \u2713 if custom logic | \u2713 if custom logic | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2713 if custom logic |\n| `/implement-ui` | \u2713 if user-facing behavior requires UI | \u2713 if UI behavior is added or changed | \u2713 if UI is affected | \u2713 if UI is in scope | \u2713 if user-facing behavior requires UI |\n| `/test-sqrl` | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always |\n| `/troubleshoot-test` | \u2713 if tests fail | \u2713 if tests fail | \u2713 if tests fail | \u2713 if tests fail | \u2713 if tests fail |\n| `/write-documentation` | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always |\n\n**Note on `/troubleshoot-test`**: this is a reactive skill. Include it as a conditional step\n(\"If tests fail after step N, use /troubleshoot-test\") rather than a mandatory step.\n\n**Note on `/from-api`**: include it whenever the requirements themselves specify the API the project must expose (GraphQL schema, REST/OpenAPI endpoints, or MCP tools). Annotate those steps with `\u2192 skill: /from-api` and order the checklist backwards from the contract: the API schema step comes before the pipeline-logic and connector steps, which depend on it.\n\n**Note on `/implement-ui`**: apply the UI assessment in Checklist Rule 12. Deferring visual design details does not make a dashboard deliverable API-only.\n\n**Note on `/data-observation` and `/handle-large-data-files`**: include both whenever the task provides source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling schemas or test data.\n\n### 9. Critical Rules\n\n- Never reference a file outside the root project.\n- Reference the requirements file for payloads, schemas, API contracts, test records and acceptance criteria.\n- Leave the names of tables, columns and functions the implementation will create to the implementation.\n- Never write SQRL, JSON, or Java statements in `## Implementation Checklist`,\n  `## Technical Decisions` or `## Data Quality & Observability`.\n- Never write a fenced code block in `## Implementation Checklist`.\n- Resolve every open question with a default in `## Assumptions`, tagged `[impact: ...]`.\n- Leave every checklist box unchecked: each one is written `- [ ]`.\n- Write only the single plan file; every other file stays untouched.\n\n### 10. Response\n\nRespond with exactly one line: the absolute path of the file you wrote.\nNothing else \u2014 no explanation, no summary.\n\n\n## Other projects in this repository (read-only)\n\nThis repository contains 3 other SQRL director(ies) besides your project. They are mounted **READ-ONLY**: you can read them for reference, but you cannot and must not modify them, and you must never copy their files into your project.\n\nA full inventory \u2014 paths, READMEs, SQRL file lists and package summaries \u2014 is in `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md`. **Read it before planning or implementing.**\n\n**Shared module(s) \u2014 investigate these FIRST:**\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 declared under script.include by customer\n\n**Other project(s) \u2014 read after the shared module(s):**\n- `customer` (project)\n- `lending` (project)\n\nFor each: read its `README.md` first if it has one, then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`. **Never read their `build/` directories** \u2014 that is compiler output and is deliberately excluded from the inventory.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```", "session_id": null}
{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:44:55.426180+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "session", "version": 3, "id": "codeagent0b784a823a5149718329c752809362de", "cwd": "/workspace/loan-payments", "source_timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:44:55.390Z"}
{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:44:55.427714+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "agent_start"}
{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:44:55.429253+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_start"}
{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:44:55.429542+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "system", "content": "", "sections": {"preamble": "You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.", "tools": "<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- grep: Search file contents for patterns (respects .gitignore)\n- find: Find files by glob pattern (respects .gitignore)\n- ls: List directory contents\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>", "rules": "<rules>\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>", "docs": "<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /usr/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>", "addendum": "<addendum>\nYou are a DataSQRL planning and Q&A expert. Your job is to investigate a DataSQRL project\nand either answer a question or produce a structured implementation plan.\n\n## What You Do NOT Do\n\n- Do NOT run compile or test commands (`cmd.sh`, `mvn`, `docker`, etc.)\n- Do NOT modify any `.sqrl`, `.json`, `.graphqls`, `.graphql`, or source files\n- Do NOT create files other than the single plan file you are explicitly told to write\n- Do NOT write SQRL, JSON config or Java statements in the plan's `## Implementation Checklist`\n  or `## Technical Decisions`\n- Do NOT write a fenced code block in the plan's `## Implementation Checklist`\n- Any referenced files must be in the project.\n\n## What You Do\n\nYou investigate, reason, and write exactly one output:\n- For a question: your answer as text\n- For an implementation request: a structured plan written to the plan-file path given in your prompt (a single self-contained ADR whose `## Implementation Checklist` uses `- [ ]` checkboxes)\n\n\n## Step 1: Read the Workspace\n\nBefore doing anything else, understand the repository around you and the current project state:\n\n1. **Survey the repository.** Your project can sit inside a repo containing other SQRL projects and shared modules (typically a data catalog). Investigate shared modules/data catalogs first, then the other projects. \nEverything outside your own project is mounted **READ-ONLY**: never modify it, never copy its files into your project, and never read another project's `build/` directory (compiler output \u2014 deliberately excluded from the inventory).\n2. Read all `.sqrl` files to understand the data model and pipeline logic\n3. Read all `package.json` files to understand configuration and engines\n4. Read `README.md` if it exists\n5. Read the recent `adr/plan_*.md` and `adr/requirements_*.md` (filenames are timestamped) to understand the latest decisions and what has already been implemented.\n\n## Step 2: Read the Available Skills\n\nThe complete text of every DataSQRL skill is loaded in your system prompt under \"DataSQRL skills (complete)\". It states the instructions and rules that apply when a DataSQRL pipeline or data catalog is built. Use it to:\n\n- classify the request along both axes (scenario and task type),\n- select an architecture whose constructs exist in DataSQRL,\n- identify the constraints that determine a plan's shape: which tables are exposed as mutations,\n  where operator state is unbounded, which connector applies per environment, which windowing\n  satisfies a stated freshness target,\n- annotate each checklist step with the skill that executes it (e.g. `\u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl`).\n\nCode examples in the skills are inputs to the implementation agent, which compiles them.\n\n`/opt/agent/skills/<name>/` holds reference pages excluded from the loaded corpus (`functions/`, `connectors/`, `formats/`, `guides/`). Read one when a design decision depends on its content.\n\n## Output Constraints\n\n- For questions: respond with your answer inline. Write no files.\n- For implementation plans: write ONLY to the plan-file path specified in your prompt, with every\n  checklist box left unchecked (`- [ ]`). Confirm completion by responding with exactly the path\n  you wrote to, nothing else.\n\n\n# Task context (persistent \u2014 keep these in mind across compaction)\n\n## Plan Section Content Rule\n\n`## Implementation Checklist`, `## Technical Decisions` and\n`## Data Quality & Observability` sections are short prose: they state what each step\nachieves and why, cite the requirements it serves, and carry the `\u2192 skill:` that supplies\nthe syntax at implementation phase, a downstreaming phase. The names of tables, columns and\nfunctions the implementation creates are left to it.\n\nCritical rules:\n\n- Never write SQRL, JSON, or Java statements in `## Implementation Checklist`, `## Technical Decisions` or `## Data Quality & Observability`.\n- Never write a fenced code block in `## Implementation Checklist`.\n- Any referenced files must be in the project.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```\n\n## Repository inventory\nThis repo contains other SQRL projects/modules. `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md` lists them (paths, READMEs, SQRL files, package summaries) \u2014 read it before deciding how to build. They are mounted READ-ONLY: never edit them, never copy their files into this project (include shared modules via `script.include` in `*-package.json` files instead), and never read their `build/` directories.\n\n## DataSQRL skills\n\nThe DataSQRL skills available. Read the ones your work touches before you\nwrite or change anything, and open the reference pages they point to.\n\n### /build-catalog \u2014 Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.\n### /configure-sqrl \u2014 Use when configuring a DataSQRL project's `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.\n### /data-observation \u2014 Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.\n### /design-api \u2014 Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.\n### /from-api \u2014 Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).\n### /handle-large-data-files \u2014 Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.\n### /implement-sqrl \u2014 Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.\n### /implement-udf \u2014 Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.\n### /implement-ui \u2014 Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.\n### /init-sqrl \u2014 Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\n### /manage-connector \u2014 Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.\n### /monitor-pipeline \u2014 Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline's monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.\n### /optimize-deployment \u2014 Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.<flink|postgres|vertx>.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.\n### /test-sqrl \u2014 Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.\n### /troubleshoot-test \u2014 Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.\n### /write-documentation \u2014 Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.\n</addendum>", "project_context": "<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"/workspace/loan-payments/AGENTS.md\">\nYou are a DataSQRL planning and Q&A expert. Your job is to investigate a DataSQRL project\nand either answer a question or produce a structured implementation plan.\n\n## What You Do NOT Do\n\n- Do NOT run compile or test commands (`cmd.sh`, `mvn`, `docker`, etc.)\n- Do NOT modify any `.sqrl`, `.json`, `.graphqls`, `.graphql`, or source files\n- Do NOT create files other than the single plan file you are explicitly told to write\n- Do NOT write SQRL, JSON config or Java statements in the plan's `## Implementation Checklist`\n  or `## Technical Decisions`\n- Do NOT write a fenced code block in the plan's `## Implementation Checklist`\n- Any referenced files must be in the project.\n\n## What You Do\n\nYou investigate, reason, and write exactly one output:\n- For a question: your answer as text\n- For an implementation request: a structured plan written to the plan-file path given in your prompt (a single self-contained ADR whose `## Implementation Checklist` uses `- [ ]` checkboxes)\n\n\n## Step 1: Read the Workspace\n\nBefore doing anything else, understand the repository around you and the current project state:\n\n1. **Survey the repository.** Your project can sit inside a repo containing other SQRL projects and shared modules (typically a data catalog). Investigate shared modules/data catalogs first, then the other projects. \nEverything outside your own project is mounted **READ-ONLY**: never modify it, never copy its files into your project, and never read another project's `build/` directory (compiler output \u2014 deliberately excluded from the inventory).\n2. Read all `.sqrl` files to understand the data model and pipeline logic\n3. Read all `package.json` files to understand configuration and engines\n4. Read `README.md` if it exists\n5. Read the recent `adr/plan_*.md` and `adr/requirements_*.md` (filenames are timestamped) to understand the latest decisions and what has already been implemented.\n\n## Step 2: Read the Available Skills\n\nThe complete text of every DataSQRL skill is loaded in your system prompt under \"DataSQRL skills (complete)\". It states the instructions and rules that apply when a DataSQRL pipeline or data catalog is built. Use it to:\n\n- classify the request along both axes (scenario and task type),\n- select an architecture whose constructs exist in DataSQRL,\n- identify the constraints that determine a plan's shape: which tables are exposed as mutations,\n  where operator state is unbounded, which connector applies per environment, which windowing\n  satisfies a stated freshness target,\n- annotate each checklist step with the skill that executes it (e.g. `\u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl`).\n\nCode examples in the skills are inputs to the implementation agent, which compiles them.\n\n`/opt/agent/skills/<name>/` holds reference pages excluded from the loaded corpus (`functions/`, `connectors/`, `formats/`, `guides/`). Read one when a design decision depends on its content.\n\n## Output Constraints\n\n- For questions: respond with your answer inline. Write no files.\n- For implementation plans: write ONLY to the plan-file path specified in your prompt, with every\n  checklist box left unchecked (`- [ ]`). Confirm completion by responding with exactly the path\n  you wrote to, nothing else.\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>", "skills": "<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nWhen a skill file references a relative path, resolve it against the skill directory (parent of SKILL.md / dirname of the path) and use that absolute path in tool commands.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>build-catalog</name>\n    <description>Use when creating or updating a data catalog that defines sources, sinks, and data products used by multiple implementations.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/build-catalog/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>configure-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when configuring a DataSQRL project&apos;s `*-package.json` files. Use for engine selection (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg and its query engines DuckDB, Snowflake, Spark SQL, Redshift, Trino), API protocols, compiler options, runtime settings, or deployment/infrastructure settings.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>data-observation</name>\n    <description>Use when a task provides source, sample, or test data files to inspect before modeling schemas, connectors, writing test data or implementing anything. Ensures you look at the real data (size, format, values) instead of guessing from filenames, READMEs, or prior knowledge.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/data-observation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>design-api</name>\n    <description>Use when designing or modifying the API exposed by a DataSQRL data pipeline. Use for GraphQL, REST, or MCP endpoints, access control, or data serving requirements. Use for adjusting exposed GraphQL schema.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/design-api/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>from-api</name>\n    <description>Use when requirements define an API schema or specification that the resulting DataSQRL pipeline should expose. Use when requirements (partially) define GraphQL schema, REST endpoints (e.g. Swagger or OpenAPI), or MCP tools with interface or result schema (JSON).</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/from-api/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>handle-large-data-files</name>\n    <description>Use when the workspace contains a large data file, or when a compile/test run is blocked with LARGE_DATA_BLOCKED. Used to sample the large files down or exclude them so that DataSQRL compiles seamlessly.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/handle-large-data-files/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when writing or editing DataSQRL scripts (.sqrl) for data processing, transformation, aggregation, or serving logic. Use for any pipeline logic implementation. Contains SQRL language spec.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-udf</name>\n    <description>Use when SQRL/SQL alone cannot implement required logic. Use to create custom scalar, aggregate, or table functions in Java.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-udf/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>implement-ui</name>\n    <description>Use for building simple user interfaces that interact with the API of a DataSQRL pipeline. Use when asked for an interface, UI, SPA, dashboard, or charts for a DataSQRL pipeline.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/implement-ui/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>init-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/init-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>manage-connector</name>\n    <description>Use when connecting to external data systems for ingestion or export in DataSQRL. Use for Kafka, filesystem, database, Iceberg, or other connector configuration.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/manage-connector/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>monitor-pipeline</name>\n    <description>Use when reviewing a DataSQRL pipeline&apos;s monitoring needs or implementing data-quality captures and observability statistics. Decide which data quality and monitoring tables are useful and how to build them.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/monitor-pipeline/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>optimize-deployment</name>\n    <description>Use when planning and implementing a DataSQRL project. Use when user specifically mentions cost or sizing. This includes creating or editing a `*-package.json`, adding an engine or an environment, and setting `engines.&lt;flink|postgres|vertx&gt;.deployment` sizes, CPU, replicas or disks. Also use when asked to reduce cloud cost or right-size a deployment.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/optimize-deployment/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>test-sqrl</name>\n    <description>Use when adding or updating tests for a DataSQRL pipeline. Use after implementing features to verify correctness.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/test-sqrl/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>troubleshoot-test</name>\n    <description>Use when a DataSQRL test fails unexpectedly or produces wrong results. Use when previous fix attempts did not resolve the issue. Use for complex DataSQRL compile-time errors or when a previous attempt of fixing a compile time error did not work.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/troubleshoot-test/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>write-documentation</name>\n    <description>Use when writing or updating project documentation, README files, or API documentation for a DataSQRL project.</description>\n    <location>/opt/agent/skills/write-documentation/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>", "cwd": "<cwd>\n/workspace/loan-payments\n</cwd>"}, "timestamp": 1795509895426, "toolsAdded": [{"name": "read", "description": "Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"}, "offset": {"type": "number", "description": "Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of lines to read"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "bash", "description": "Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["command"], "properties": {"command": {"type": "string", "description": "Shell command to execute"}, "timeout": {"type": "number", "description": "Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "edit", "description": "Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path", "edits"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"}, "edits": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["oldText", "newText"], "properties": {"oldText": {"type": "string", "description": "Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."}, "newText": {"type": "string", "description": "Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}}, "description": "One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "write", "description": "Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["path", "content"], "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"}, "content": {"type": "string", "description": "Content to write to the file"}}}, "constrainedSampling": {"type": "json_schema", "strict": "prefer"}}, {"name": "grep", "description": "Search file contents for a pattern. Returns matching lines with file paths and line numbers. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 100 matches or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Long lines are truncated to 500 chars.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["pattern"], "properties": {"pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "Search pattern (regex or literal string)"}, "path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory or file to search (default: current directory)"}, "glob": {"type": "string", "description": "Filter files by glob pattern, e.g. '*.ts' or '**/*.spec.ts'"}, "ignoreCase": {"type": "boolean", "description": "Case-insensitive search (default: false)"}, "literal": {"type": "boolean", "description": "Treat pattern as literal string instead of regex (default: false)"}, "context": {"type": "number", "description": "Number of lines to show before and after each match (default: 0)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of matches to return (default: 100)"}}}}, {"name": "find", "description": "Search for files by glob pattern. Returns matching file paths relative to the search directory. Respects .gitignore. Output is truncated to 1000 results or 50KB (whichever is hit first).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["pattern"], "properties": {"pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "Glob pattern to match files, e.g. '*.ts', '**/*.json', or 'src/**/*.spec.ts'"}, "path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory to search in (default: current directory)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of results (default: 1000)"}}}}, {"name": "ls", "description": "List directory contents. Returns entries sorted alphabetically, with '/' suffix for directories. Includes dotfiles. Output is truncated to 500 entries or 50KB (whichever is hit first).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"path": {"type": "string", "description": "Directory to list (default: current directory)"}, "limit": {"type": "number", "description": "Maximum number of entries to return (default: 500)"}}}}]}}
{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:44:55.430914+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# DataSQRL Planning & Q&A\n\n## Request\n\n---\ntype: user-requirements\n---\n\n# Requirements: Loan Payments (personal loans)\n\n## Goal\n\nGive each personal-loan customer an API to look up their own loans: the payment summary of each\nloan and the payments of each loan. The customer signs in through the SSO, and the API shows only\nthe loans of the `customer_id` in their JWT.\n\n## Scope\n\n**In scope:**\n\n- A new DataSQRL project in `loan-payments/` that reads the finance data catalog\n  (`data-catalog/`, a git submodule at the repository root) and does not change it.\n- The table `LoanPayments`: one row per payment of a personal loan, with the customer id and the\n  loan purpose from the loan record.\n- The table `LoanPaymentSummary`: one row per personal loan, with payment counts and totals.\n- An API with the three lookups in [API surface](#api-surface), authorized by the JWT claim\n  `customer_id`.\n- Tests that use the test data of the catalog.\n- A `test` and a `prod` environment, with the same layout as the sibling project `lending/`\n  (`lending360-shared-package.json`, `lending360-test-package.json`, `lending360-prod-package.json`).\n\n**Out of scope:**\n\n- Auto loans and auto-loan payments. The catalog table `Loan_Payment` holds both, and this project\n  keeps only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'`.\n- Mortgages, credit cards and delinquency events.\n- Payment submission or any other write to the source systems. The API has no mutations.\n- Staff or admin access to the loans of other customers.\n- Changes to the data catalog, its schemas or its test data.\n- A web UI.\n\n## Functional requirements\n\n- **FR-1**: Read the personal loans from the catalog table `Personal_Loan` and the payments from\n  the catalog table `Loan_Payment` (dataset `lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans`).\n- **FR-2**: Keep the latest version of each personal loan (key `loan_id`) and of each payment\n  (key `payment_id`), by `source_updated_at`. Both sources are CDC streams.\n- **FR-3**: Keep only the payments of personal loans: `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` and a `loan_id`\n  that exists in `Personal_Loan`.\n- **FR-4**: `LoanPayments` has one row per `payment_id`. Each row has the payment fields listed in\n  [Transformations](#transformations-and-aggregations), plus `customer_id`\n  (= `Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id`) and `loan_purpose` from the loan record.\n- **FR-5**: `LoanPayments` keeps payments of every status (`APPLIED`, `PENDING`, `RETURNED`,\n  `REVERSED`) so that a customer sees their returned payments.\n- **FR-6**: `LoanPaymentSummary` has one row per personal loan, with `loan_id`, `customer_id`,\n  `loan_purpose` and these values:\n  - `applied_payment_count`: the number of payments with `payment_status = 'APPLIED'`.\n  - `returned_payment_count`: the number of payments with `payment_status = 'RETURNED'`.\n  - `total_paid_cents`: the sum of `payment_amount_cents`.\n  - `principal_paid_cents`: the sum of `principal_cents`.\n  - `interest_paid_cents`: the sum of `interest_cents`.\n  - `late_fees_paid_cents`: the sum of `late_fee_cents`.\n  - `last_payment_date`: the latest `applied_date`.\n- **FR-7**: The totals and `last_payment_date` in FR-6 use only the payments with\n  `payment_status = 'APPLIED'`. A `RETURNED`, `PENDING` or `REVERSED` payment adds nothing to them.\n- **FR-8**: When a payment changes status (for example `APPLIED` to `RETURNED`), the summary of its\n  loan uses only the latest status.\n- **FR-9**: Query `LoanPaymentSummary` by `loan_id`.\n- **FR-10**: Query the `LoanPayments` of a loan by `loan_id`.\n- **FR-11**: Query all loans of a customer, each with its summary, by `customer_id`.\n- **FR-12**: Every query returns only rows whose `customer_id` equals the JWT claim `customer_id`.\n  A request for a loan of another customer returns no rows.\n- **FR-13**: A request without a valid JWT, or with a JWT that has no `customer_id` claim, is\n  rejected.\n\n## Sources\n\nBoth sources are catalog tables that the project imports. The project manages no data itself.\n\n### `Personal_Loan` (catalog)\n\n- **Ownership**: external. The consumer lending system publishes it. The project only reads it.\n- **Catalog files**: schema `data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/consumer_loans.sqrl`\n  (`Personal_Loan_schema`), connectors `consumer_loans-test.sqrl` and `consumer_loans-prod.sqrl`.\n- **Transport**: test: file `testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl`. prod: Kafka topic\n  `consumer_loans.personal_loan` on `${LENDING_KAFKA_BROKERS}`.\n- **Format**: JSON, ISO-8601 timestamps. No schema registry.\n- **Sample payload** (test data):\n\n  ```json\n  {\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-023\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-066\",\n   \"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-28229\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"MAJOR_PURCHASE\",\n   \"original_amount_cents\":1740000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1566,\n   \"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\n   \"monthly_payment_cents\":84913,\"funding_date\":\"2020-09-11\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-10-11\",\n   \"maturity_date\":\"2022-09-11\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-09-04\",\n   \"origination_fee_cents\":34800,\"credit_score_at_origination\":644,\"dti_at_origination\":24.62,\n   \"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\n   \"origination_channel\":\"BRANCH\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\n   \"source_updated_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:40:57Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:44:22Z\"}\n  ```\n\n  Nullable fields used by this project: `loan_purpose`. `loan_id` and\n  `primary_borrower_customer_id` are `NOT NULL`.\n- **Volume and rate**: test data: 170 loans, 142 customers. Production volume is an open question.\n- **Delivery guarantee**: at-least-once (Kafka, `kafka-safe` connector). Replay from the earliest\n  offset is possible.\n- **Duplicates and ordering**: a CDC stream. Several versions of a `loan_id` can arrive. The latest\n  `source_updated_at` wins.\n- **Updates**: CDC updates to prior state (for example `ACTIVE` to `PAID_OFF`).\n\n### `Loan_Payment` (catalog)\n\n- **Ownership**: external, the same consumer lending system.\n- **Catalog files**: schema `Loan_Payment_schema` in the same `consumer_loans.sqrl`, with the same\n  connector files.\n- **Transport**: test: file `testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl`. prod: Kafka topic\n  `consumer_loans.loan_payment`.\n- **Format**: JSON, ISO-8601 timestamps.\n- **Sample payload** (test data, a returned payment):\n\n  ```json\n  {\"payment_id\":\"LPMT-046\",\"loan_id\":\"PL-115\",\"loan_type\":\"PERSONAL\",\"payment_amount_cents\":57760,\n   \"principal_cents\":37108,\"interest_cents\":20652,\"late_fee_cents\":0,\"other_fees_cents\":0,\n   \"payment_type\":\"REGULAR\",\"payment_status\":\"RETURNED\",\"due_date\":\"2025-10-08\",\n   \"received_date\":\"2025-10-05\",\"applied_date\":\"2025-10-05\",\"payment_method\":\"ACH\",\n   \"principal_balance_after_cents\":1632892,\"days_past_due_at_payment\":0,\n   \"confirmation_number\":\"ACH-20251005-00046\",\"return_reason_code\":\"R01\",\n   \"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2025-10-05T14:36:25Z\",\n   \"ingested_at\":\"2025-10-05T14:39:42Z\"}\n  ```\n\n  Nullable fields: `principal_cents`, `interest_cents`, `late_fee_cents`, `other_fees_cents`,\n  `received_date`, `applied_date`, `payment_method`, `principal_balance_after_cents`,\n  `days_past_due_at_payment`, `confirmation_number`, `return_reason_code`.\n- **Volume and rate**: test data: 1,942 payments. 1,010 are personal (1,001 `APPLIED`,\n  9 `RETURNED`). The test data has no `PENDING` or `REVERSED` payment.\n- **Delivery guarantee**: at-least-once.\n- **Duplicates and ordering**: a CDC stream. Deduplicate on `payment_id`, the latest\n  `source_updated_at` wins.\n- **Updates**: CDC. A payment can change status after it arrives.\n\n## Time semantics\n\n- **Event-time field**: `ingested_at` (`TIMESTAMP_LTZ(3)`, UTC). The catalog connectors set the\n  watermark on it: 10 seconds in test, 30 seconds in prod. The project uses these watermarks as\n  they are.\n- **Version order**: `source_updated_at` picks the latest version of a loan or a payment.\n- **Lateness**: a payment that arrives late still counts. The summary is not windowed, so a late\n  payment updates the summary of its loan.\n- **Processing time**: not used.\n\n## Entities and keys\n\n| Entity | Key | Source | Relations |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Personal loan | `loan_id` | catalog `Personal_Loan` | belongs to one customer (`primary_borrower_customer_id`) |\n| Payment | `payment_id` | catalog `Loan_Payment` | belongs to one loan (`loan_id`) |\n| Customer | `customer_id` | the JWT claim, and `Personal_Loan.primary_borrower_customer_id` | has zero or more personal loans |\n\nThe `customer_id` of a payment comes from its loan, not from the payment record.\n\n## Transformations and aggregations\n\n1. **Latest loans**: the latest version of each `Personal_Loan` by `source_updated_at`, key\n   `loan_id`. All loan statuses stay (`ACTIVE`, `PAID_OFF`, `CHARGED_OFF`, `SETTLED`).\n2. **Latest payments**: the latest version of each `Loan_Payment` by `source_updated_at`, key\n   `payment_id`, filtered to `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'`.\n3. **`LoanPayments`**: latest payments joined to latest loans on `loan_id` (inner join). Columns:\n   `payment_id`, `loan_id`, `customer_id`, `loan_purpose`, `payment_amount_cents`,\n   `principal_cents`, `interest_cents`, `late_fee_cents`, `other_fees_cents`, `payment_type`,\n   `payment_status`, `due_date`, `received_date`, `applied_date`, `payment_method`,\n   `principal_balance_after_cents`, `days_past_due_at_payment`, `confirmation_number`,\n   `return_reason_code`. Default order: `due_date` descending, then `payment_id`.\n4. **`LoanPaymentSummary`**: group `LoanPayments` by `loan_id`, with the values of FR-6 and the\n   filter rule of FR-7. Window: none (unbounded, updated on each change). A loan without payments\n   gets a row with counts and sums of 0 and a null `last_payment_date` (see Open Questions).\n\nAmounts stay in cents (`BIGINT`), the same unit as the catalog.\n\n## Results\n\n### `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`\n\n- **Kind**: API (served from the database of the project).\n- **Consumers**: personal-loan customers, through the client app of the bank.\n- **Authorization**: authorized. The JWT from the SSO carries the claim `customer_id`. Every query\n  filters on `customer_id = <claim customer_id>`. No other claim gives access.\n\n## API surface\n\n- **Queries** (all filtered by the JWT claim `customer_id`, see FR-12):\n  - `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id)`: the summary of one loan. Returns 0 or 1 row.\n  - `LoanPayments(loan_id)`: the payments of one loan, newest due date first, paged\n    (`limit`, `offset`).\n  - Loans of a customer: all personal loans of the signed-in customer, each with its summary.\n    The `customer_id` comes from the JWT claim, so the caller cannot ask for another customer.\n- **Mutations**: none.\n- **Subscriptions**: none.\n- **Protocols**: GraphQL, REST and MCP, the same as the sibling project `lending/`.\n- **Access**: every query needs a valid JWT with the claim `customer_id`.\n\n## Test data and acceptance criteria\n\n- **Test data**: the catalog test data in\n  `data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/`\n  (`consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl`, `consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl`), read through the\n  catalog test connectors. The project adds no source data of its own.\n- **Edge cases in that data**:\n  - Auto-loan payments (932 rows) that the filter must remove.\n  - 9 `RETURNED` personal payments, on loans `PL-023`, `PL-061`, `PL-062`, `PL-068`, `PL-071`,\n    `PL-079`, `PL-108`, `PL-115`, `PL-121`.\n  - 26 applied personal payments with `late_fee_cents > 0`.\n  - 35 personal loans without payments (for example `PL-011`, `PL-014`).\n  - 23 customers with more than one personal loan (for example `CUST-060` with `PL-006`,\n    `PL-007`, `PL-022`, `PL-033`).\n  - Loans in every status (`ACTIVE` 114, `PAID_OFF` 51, `CHARGED_OFF` 4, `SETTLED` 1).\n- **Acceptance criteria**:\n  - *(FR-1, FR-3)* `LoanPayments` has 1,010 rows. No row has a `loan_id` that starts with `AL-`.\n  - *(FR-2)* `LoanPayments` has one row per `payment_id`, and `LoanPaymentSummary` has one row\n    per `loan_id`.\n  - *(FR-4)* Each `LoanPayments` row has the `customer_id` and `loan_purpose` of its loan in\n    `Personal_Loan`.\n  - *(FR-5)* `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` includes payment `LPMT-046` with\n    `payment_status = \"RETURNED\"` and `return_reason_code = \"R01\"`.\n  - *(FR-6, FR-7)* For `PL-115`, `returned_payment_count = 1`, and `total_paid_cents` equals the\n    sum of `payment_amount_cents` of its `APPLIED` payments only (it excludes the 57,760 of\n    `LPMT-046`). The planner computes the expected values from the test data.\n  - *(FR-6)* For a loan with applied late fees, `late_fees_paid_cents` equals the sum of their\n    `late_fee_cents`.\n  - *(FR-6)* For `PL-011` (no payments), the summary row has counts and sums of 0 and a null\n    `last_payment_date`.\n  - *(FR-8)* Not testable with the catalog test data, because no payment changes status in it.\n    The plan says how it covers this rule.\n  - *(FR-9, FR-12)* With a JWT for `CUST-439` (the customer of `PL-115`), `LoanPaymentSummary(loan_id: \"PL-115\")`\n    returns 1 row. With a JWT for `CUST-060`, the same query returns 0 rows.\n  - *(FR-10, FR-12)* With a JWT for `CUST-060`, `LoanPayments(loan_id: \"PL-115\")` returns 0 rows.\n  - *(FR-11)* With a JWT for `CUST-060`, the customer query returns the 4 loans `PL-006`, `PL-007`,\n    `PL-022`, `PL-033`, each with its summary.\n  - *(FR-13)* A request without a JWT gets an authorization error.\n  - Snapshot tests store the API responses above under the test folder of the project.\n\n## Non-functional requirements\n\n- **Freshness**: a new or changed payment shows in `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary` within\n  1 minute of its arrival in Kafka (see Open Questions).\n- **API latency SLA**: p99 < 200 ms for each query, at up to 50 requests/second (see Open Questions).\n- **Workload**: unknown in production (see Open Questions). The test data has 170 loans and\n  1,010 personal payments.\n- **Budget**: none stated (see Open Questions).\n- **Reliability**: no data loss. After a failure, the pipeline restarts from its last checkpoint\n  and replays from Kafka.\n- **Retention**: the API keeps all payments of all personal loans, with no expiry.\n- **PII and sensitive fields**: `customer_id` is tagged `@pii`. The amounts are `@npi`. The API\n  shows them only to the customer that owns the loan (FR-12). The API leaves out `loan_number`\n  (`@npi @mask`), the account ids, the credit score and the DTI ratio.\n- **Compliance**: GLBA, GDPR, CCPA, the same as the catalog dataset. No new storage outside the\n  database of the project.\n- **Availability**: no target stated. The default deployment of DataSQRL Cloud applies.\n\n## Hard constraints\n\n- Use the catalog tables `Personal_Loan` and `Loan_Payment` from `data-catalog/` through\n  `script.include`, the same way as `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`. Do not copy or\n  re-declare them. Reason: the user asked to use the finance data catalog.\n- Use the catalog test data for the tests. Reason: the user asked for it.\n- Use the table names `LoanPayments` and `LoanPaymentSummary`. Reason: the user named them.\n- Authorize with the JWT claim `customer_id` from the SSO. Reason: the user stated it.\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- **Freshness**: how fast must a payment show in the API? Recommended default: *within 1 minute,\n  with the streaming Flink pipeline of the sibling `lending/`*. Impact if wrong: high.\n- **JWT validation**: which signing algorithm, issuer, audience and key does the SSO use?\n  Recommended default: *HS256 with a fixed test secret in `test`, and the key from an environment\n  variable (for example `${JWT_SECRET}`) in `prod`*. Impact if wrong: high.\n- **Production workload**: how many loans and payments per day, and how many API requests per\n  second? Recommended default: *up to 1 million personal loans, 50,000 payments per day, and\n  50 requests/second peak*. Impact if wrong: medium.\n- **Last payment date**: is it the latest `applied_date` of an applied payment, or the latest\n  `received_date`? Recommended default: *the latest `applied_date` of an `APPLIED` payment*.\n  Impact if wrong: low.\n- **Total amount paid**: is it `payment_amount_cents` (the full payment, which includes fees)?\n  Recommended default: *yes, the sum of `payment_amount_cents` of applied payments*. Impact if\n  wrong: low.\n- **Loans without payments**: does `LoanPaymentSummary` show a row for a loan with no payments?\n  Recommended default: *yes, with counts and sums of 0 and a null `last_payment_date`*. Impact if\n  wrong: medium.\n- **Loan status**: does the API show loans in every status, or only `ACTIVE` loans? Recommended\n  default: *every status*. Impact if wrong: medium.\n- **Loan details in the customer query**: does the customer query show loan fields other than the\n  summary (for example `loan_status`, `current_balance_cents`)? Recommended default: *add\n  `loan_status`, `original_amount_cents`, `current_balance_cents`, `monthly_payment_cents` and\n  `next_payment_due_date`*. Impact if wrong: low.\n- **API protocols**: GraphQL, REST and MCP, or GraphQL only? Recommended default: *all three, the\n  same as `lending/`*. Impact if wrong: low.\n- **Budget**: what may the pipeline cost per month? Recommended default: *the smallest DataSQRL\n  Cloud deployment size*. Impact if wrong: low.\n\nRequirements file: `adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`\n\n## Classify the Request\n\nBased on the workspace you have read and the request above, determine which applies:\n\n**QUESTION** - the user is asking a factual or conceptual question about DataSQRL, SQRL syntax, or this project. There is no intent to implement or change anything.\n\n**IMPLEMENTATION REQUEST** \u2014 the user wants to build, extend, or change a DataSQRL project. Even if the request is vague or high-level, if the intent is to produce working code, treat it as an implementation request.\n\nThese are mutually exclusive. Choose one and follow only that branch below.\n\n---\n\n## Branch A: QUESTION\n\nAnswer the question concisely and accurately. Use the workspace context and skill documentation you have read to inform your answer.\nDo NOT write any files. Do NOT generate an ADR. Your final response IS the answer.\n\n---\n\n## Branch B: IMPLEMENTATION REQUEST\n\nWork through the sections below in order: survey the repository, classify the request along two axes, write the plan file, and finish with the single-line response.\n\nThe plan is a short, high-level decomposition of the work into steps that a developer can review in a few minutes. Downstream implementation agent is going to read the generated plan and puts the details as it builds. Specific details like table names, record-level test values are the decisions of the downstream implementation agent.\n\n### 1. Survey the Repository\n\nA project can live inside a repository that holds **other SQRL projects and shared modules** (most often a data catalog). Survey them before classifying the request.\n\nHigh level repo contents (paths, READMEs, SQRL file lists, package summaries) are placed in `repo-inventory.md`. **Read that file first**, then deepen selectively:\n\n1. **Data catalogs and shared modules first.** They define the source tables everything else builds on. Read the module's `README.md` if present, then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`. Note the table names, schemas and the `` template variables it expects.\n2. **Then the other projects.** with the same order. Read `README.md`, then `.sqrl`, then `package.json`. If those projects use shared module or shared data catalog, look at how they declare `script.include` in package file and how they import the catalog.\n3. **Understand upstream guarantees and existing monitoring.** For the sources this job uses, read the assertions to learn what the data guarantees. Identify raw and cleaned tables (Raw tables contain records before the relevant upstream cleanup; cleaned tables expose the result with documented guarantees.), explicitly documented exceptions to those guarantees, and existing monitoring relevant to this job. Prefer the cleaned catalog tables unless this job is responsible for cleaning or enriching raw data.\n\nHard rules while surveying:\n\n- Everything outside the project is mounted **READ-ONLY**. Never create, edit or delete anything there, and never copy their files into the current project. \n- A shared module or a sibling SQRL project can be consumed by declaring it under `script.include` in package file (namespace \u2192 `package` path to any top-level package file of the module; that file's folder and all its subfolders are loaded) and importing it by that namespace in sqrl files.\n- **Never read another project's `build/` directory.**\n- `.sqrl` and `package.json` are enough to understand a sibling SQRL project. Do not read their test data if it is not compulsory.\n\nUse what you find, during exploration, to choose/write the right steps: which shared module or catalog the project reuses instead of rebuilding, which existing files a step extends, and which existing monitoring already covers a source.\n\n### 2. Classify the Request Along Two Axes\n\nClassify the request along **two independent axes**: the **artifact type** (`scenario`) and the **task type**. These are orthogonal, determine each separately. Record both in the plan header; the reasoning behind them stays in your analysis.\n\n#### Axis 1 \u2014 Artifact type (`scenario`)\n\nThis is recorded in the ADR frontmatter as `scenario` and **drives which verifiers run** during implementation. There are exactly two values:\n\n- **`pipeline`** \u2014 a DataSQRL pipeline: ingests from sources, processes/aggregates data, and exposes an API (GraphQL/REST/MCP). Files are `.sqrl` scripts plus `*-package.json` files that configure sqrl pipeline.\n- **`catalog`** \u2014 a standalone data catalog: a shared repository of schema definitions (`{dataset}.sqrl` with `_schema` tables), per-environment connector files (`{dataset}-<env>.sqrl`), ontologies, and test data, used across multiple pipelines. Focus is schema documentation, governance, and relationship modelling, **not** pipeline transformation logic or a GraphQL API.\n\n#### Axis 2 \u2014 Task type\n\nThis shapes the plan (it selects which questions to answer below) but is **not** parsed by the system. Record it in the ADR body as `**Task**:` (not in the frontmatter). Pick the task that best matches the artifact type you chose in Axis 1.\n\nThe questions below guide your analysis. Their answers shape the checklist steps and the few decisions worth recording; they are not written into the plan one by one.\n\n##### For `scenario: pipeline`\n\n**`new-project`** \u2014 No `.sqrl` files exist; building a pipeline from scratch.\n- What are the data sources (Kafka topics, databases, files)?\n- What processing and aggregations are required?\n- What is the desired API(s) (GraphQL queries, REST, MCP tools)?\n- Which engines are needed (Flink, Postgres, Kafka, Iceberg, Vert.x, Snowflake, SparkSQL, Redshift, Trino)?\n- Which template in `/opt/datasqrl-examples/` is the closest starting point?\n- Are any custom Java functions (UDFs) required for processing logic?\n\n**`extend-project`** \u2014 `.sqrl` and `*-package.json` files already exist; adding or modifying\nfunctionality in an existing pipeline.\n- What tables, functions, or relationships need to be added or changed?\n- What is the impact on existing downstream tables, API, and tests?\n- Which existing snapshots need to be regenerated?\n- Are there naming or ordering conflicts with existing definitions?\n- Do any connectors need to be added or reconfigured?\n- Are any custom Java functions (UDFs) required for processing logic?\n\n**`connector-setup`** \u2014 Configuring data sources, sinks, or engine settings \u2014 not writing\npipeline logic.\n- Which connector type (Kafka, Iceberg, Postgres, file, etc.)?\n- What environment-specific configuration is required (dev vs. test vs. prod)?\n- Are there security or authentication settings needed?\n- Does the connector configuration need to be split across environment files?\n- Do existing SQRL scripts need to be updated to use the new connector?\n\n**`architecture-design`** \u2014 A domain problem with no technical architecture decided yet; requires\nfull design before any code is written.\n- What are the latency and throughput requirements?\n- Which engines are appropriate for each stage?\n- Where are the stateful operations and how can state growth be bounded?\n- What is the GraphQL API schema shape?\n- Are there non-incremental aggregations, or complete-snapshot file sources, that require special\n  handling (e.g. per-snapshot aggregation via a `SESSION` window to avoid double-counting;\n  bounded state)?\n- Are custom UDFs or a UI layer required?\n\n##### For `scenario: catalog`\n\n**`new-catalog`** \u2014 Creating a standalone data catalog from scratch.\n- What organizational hierarchy applies \u2014 single-team flat structure or line-of-business \u2192 team\n  hierarchy?\n- What datasets are in scope: which are source datasets (raw ingestion from external systems) vs\n  derived datasets (enriched or conformed views built on top of sources)?\n- What governance framework is required: classification levels, tagging conventions (`@pii`,\n  `@gdpr`, `@encrypt`), data ownership?\n- What environments are needed \u2014 test (filesystem connector) and/or prod (Kafka/Iceberg/other)?\n- What ontology relationships need to be defined across datasets (and across organizational\n  units)?\n- What test data is available or needs to be created for each dataset?\n- What data quality rules and constraints apply to each dataset \u2014 e.g. valid enum values,\n  mandatory fields, referential integrity, no self-references \u2014 that should be enforced as\n  `/*+test(no_rows) */` assertions in unit ontology files?\n\n**`extend-catalog`** \u2014 Adding, modifying, or reorganizing datasets, ontologies, or test data in an existing catalog.\n- Which existing line-of-business / team folder do the new or changed datasets map to?\n- What layout does the catalog use today, and does the request ask to change it? A specific layout restructure request is the only reason to move existing files. When it does ask, name the target layout, list every file that moves from its current path to its new path, and name the imports and test data paths that change with them.\n- What is the impact on existing ontology relationships and data quality assertions?\n- Do new datasets follow the catalog's established governance and tagging conventions?\n- Which existing test snapshots need to be regenerated?\n- Are there naming conflicts with existing datasets or schema tables?\n\n### 3. Self-Containment Rule\n\nDownstreaming implementation agent is going to receive the plan, and it runs inside the project, so it can read project files directly. In the plan, every specification, payload, expected value, and requirement text required to build the project is either present in the plan file or in a project file this plan references by path.\n\n- **Reference the requirements file.** The request above is saved in the project at the given path. Payloads, schemas, API contracts, test records and acceptance criteria stay in that file, and the implementation agent reads them there.\n- **Reference other project files by path.** An API schema or operations file, an existing `.sqrl` script, a `*-package.json` or a data file is referenced by its path when needed. Every file the plan references must be in the project.\n- **Point steps at requirements by their own labels.** Cite a requirement by the identifier or heading it has in the requirements source (`FR-3`, `### Source payloads`), so the implementation agent can look it up.\n\n### 4. Plan Section Content Rule\n\nPlan file sections are short prose. They say **what** must be achieved and **why**, and leave **how** to downstream implementation agent.\n\nLeave to the next stage (implementation agent): the specific details that are not written in the requirements but are going to be done like names of tables, columns, function scripts.\n\n\n### 5. Write the Plan File\n\nWrite a concise, actionable implementation plan to: `/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md`\nThe path already contains a unique timestamp. Create the plan file skeleton and then write it part by part. Do NOT modify any other file in the `adr/` directory.\n\n#### Build the file section by section\n\nBuild the plan in two stages: one Write for the skeleton, then one Edit per section. Each Edit lands its section permanently the moment it returns, so an interrupted run still leaves the finished sections on disk.\n\n1. **Skeleton.** One `Write`: frontmatter, title, the `**Scenario**` / `**Task**` / `**Date**` / `**Status**` lines, the `---` rule, and every `## ` heading in *ADR Format* order, each followed by `[PENDING: <section name>]`. Naming the section keeps every marker unique, so a later `Edit` matches exactly one place.\n2. **Fill.** One `Edit` per section, top to bottom, replacing that section's marker. Keep each `Edit` under about 100 lines of new text, and split a longer section across consecutive `Edit`s. One long `Edit` can hit the output limit and be lost causing waste of time, so make several consecutive `Edit`s in those cases.\n3. **Grow the file only through `Edit`.** A second `Write` to `/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md` replaces the whole file and rebuilds the problem this avoids.\n4. **Verify.** Before responding, re-read `/workspace/loan-payments/adr/plan_20261124-084455.md` and confirm no `[PENDING:` marker survives. A leftover marker means that section was never written: fill it now.\n\n#### Domain Identification\n\nIdentify which domain(s) apply based on the requirements and workspace content. \nMultiple domains may apply. List all that match. Use the signals below:\n\n| Domain | Apply when you see |\n|--------|--------------------|\n| `finance` | transactions, payments, portfolio, trading, balances, financial instruments |\n| `fraud` | fraud detection, anomaly detection, risk scoring, suspicious activity |\n| `healthcare` | medical records, patient data, clinical events, diagnoses, prescriptions |\n| `ecommerce` | orders, products, shopping cart, inventory, shipments, storefronts |\n| `iot` | sensor data, device telemetry, machine readings, time-series streams |\n| `analytics` | dashboards, business intelligence, KPIs, reporting, aggregated metrics |\n\nIf the requirements span multiple domains (e.g. fraud detection in e-commerce), list all that apply. If none match clearly, use an empty list.\n\n#### Data Quality & Observability Items\n\nAssess data quality and observability for this particular job, even when the user does not ask for them. Focus on problems most likely to affect its results, consumers, or downstream processing. Record concise Build, Skip, and Reuse decisions; do not enumerate every hypothetical check.\n\n1. **Trust the data catalog.** Treat catalog assertions as upstream guarantees, not a list of tests to repeat in pipelines. Only treat an assertion as unenforced when the catalog or source explicitly documents that it is not enforced on source data and must be evaluated downstream. The fact that an assertion runs in tests does not establish that exception.\n2. **Only add source data quality check when the following conditions hold:** invalid data would break this job's processing or intended result, and such data can reach the job because the relevant upstream guarantee is absent or explicitly unenforced. Prefer cleaned catalog tables. A job responsible for cleaning or enriching raw records may read the raw table and capture invalid records or count them when retaining records would be too expensive.\n3. **Focus on failures that matter to this job.** Inspect joins, filters, and derived results for credible failures that would otherwise go unnoticed. For example, capture a transaction with no matching customer at event time only when that case is possible and silently losing the transaction harms this job's result.\n4. **Only add observability for critical blind spots.** Do not require hourly counts for every source or reproduce Flink metrics. For example, counts by department can be justified when departments send independently and one department stopping would harm the result while aggregate metrics hide it.\n5. **State each Build decision as a risk, not a table.** Say what would goes wrong, where in the pipeline it shows up, who acts on it, and whether it needs captured records (data quality) or counts (observability).\n\n#### ADR Format\n\nEvery implementation plan follows this structure, with the sections in this order:\n\n```\n## Requirements Source\n## Implementation Checklist\n## Data Quality & Observability\n## Technical Decisions\n## Assumptions\n```\n\nThe block below describe each section's content as copied material.\n```markdown\n---\ndomains:\n  - <domain>  # identify and list the domain(s) based on the requirements and workspace content. Use [] if none apply\nscenario: <pipeline | catalog>   # Axis 1 \u2014 artifact type; drives verification\n---\n\n# Implementation Plan: <short descriptive title>\n\n**Scenario**: <same value as the `scenario` field in the frontmatter above: pipeline | catalog>\n**Task**: <Axis 2 task type \u2014 for pipeline: new-project | extend-project | connector-setup | architecture-design; for catalog: new-catalog | extend-catalog>\n**Date**: <UTC datetime, e.g. 2026-06-08T14:30:00Z>\n**Status**: DRAFT\n\n<One or two sentences: what is being built and what it reuses.>\n\n---\n\n## Requirements Source\n<The path of the requirements file (e.g. `adr/requirements_20260608-120000.md`), plus any other project file that carries part of the specification (an API schema, a sample data file).>\n\n## Implementation Checklist\n\n<Each step is a markdown checkbox that keeps its sequential number (numbers are referenced by `[deps: ...]`). Write EVERY box unchecked (`- [ ]`) with enumaration.>\n\nExample:\n- [ ] 1. Initialize project from nearest template [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- [ ] 2. Connect the order events source (see `### Sources` in the requirements) [deps: 1] \u2192 skill: /manage-connector\n- [ ] 3. Build the event pipeline: ingest the event mutation, and compute the hourly per-namespace rollups served by the query operations in `api/operations.graphql`. [deps: 1, 2] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n\n...\n\n## Data Quality & Observability\n<Three short lists in plain prose, without code. Empty lists are valid; say so when nothing new is needed.\n\n- **Build** \u2014 each critical risk: what would go wrong, where it shows up, why upstream guarantees and existing coverage miss it, and who acts on it.\n- **Skip** \u2014 material decisions to omit monitoring, with the reason (the catalog guarantees it, existing metrics suffice, nobody would act).\n- **Reuse** \u2014 existing monitoring in the catalog or a sibling project that covers a need, by path.>\n\n## Technical Decisions\n<A few bullets, one or two sentences each: the architectural choices the requirements leave open (engine selection, ingestion or watermark strategy, snapshot-aggregation strategy, how the API is exposed, catalog layout), each with the constraint that determined it. Example: \"hourly rollups use a cumulative window, which emits the open hour within the 30 s freshness target; a tumbling window emits only at the hour boundary\">\n\n## Assumptions\n<Every unknown the planning agent encountered, along with the concrete default it chose. Make a decision and state it here, because the implementation agent proceeds from these assumptions and a question left open stalls its progress. Format each assumption as:\n\"**<topic>**: Assumed <decision>. Rationale: <one sentence why this default is reasonable.> [impact: high|medium|low]\"\n\nThe `[impact: ...]` tag rates how much the implementation would change if the assumption turned out wrong:\n- **high** \u2014 a wrong guess forces material rework: schema shape, primary keys, source/connector type, connector or import paths (e.g. `data_catalog.<lob>.<dataset>.<table>`), data semantics, or scope inclusion/exclusion.\n- **medium** \u2014 a localized change (a single table or one endpoint).\n- **low** \u2014 trivially adjustable later\n\nMark only the genuinely load-bearing assumptions `high`>\n```\n\n### 6. Checklist Rules\n\nThe implementation checklist is the most important section of the plan. It also serves as the\nimplementation agent's durable progress tracker: each step is a markdown checkbox the\nimplementation agent marks off as it goes, and re-reads to resume after a context compaction.\n\n1. **Every step is an unchecked checkbox** that starts with `- [ ] ` before its number:\n   `- [ ] 1. ...`, `- [ ] 2. ...`. Write the 4-state legend line (shown in the ADR Format\n   template) directly under the `## Implementation Checklist` heading so the plan file is\n   self-describing. Leave every box unchecked and never pre-mark a box `- [*]`, `- [x]`, or\n   `- [~]`. Those states are filled in later, only by the implementation agent as it works:\n   it marks a box `- [*]` when it starts a step and `- [x]` once that step compiles/passes.\n2. **Number every step** sequentially starting from 1, immediately after the checkbox. The\n   number is load-bearing \u2014 `[deps: ...]` references it \u2014 so keep the numbering contiguous.\n3. **Dependency annotation** is required on every step:\n   - `[deps: none]` \u2014 no dependencies, can run immediately\n   - `[deps: 1, 3]` \u2014 depends on steps 1 and 3 completing first\n4. **Condition annotation** for steps that only execute under certain circumstances:\n   - `[cond: <condition>]` \u2014 the step runs only when the stated condition is true\n   - Two kinds of conditions:\n     - **Reactive**: triggered by something going wrong during execution \u2014\n       e.g., `[cond: tests fail after step 5]`\n     - **Requirement-dependent**: triggered by a feature need the planner could not fully\n       determine \u2014 e.g., `[cond: custom Java processing logic required]`\n   - Omit `[cond]` entirely for unconditional steps (they always run)\n5. **Skill annotation** where a skill directly applies:\n   - Append skills as `\u2192 skill: /skill-name-1, /skill-name-2` after all other annotations\n   - Omit for steps that are general actions (reading, cleanup, git operations)\n6. **Steps that share the same dependencies and do not depend on each other** should be listed\n   as separate top-level checkbox lines (not a nested sub-list). They are candidates for parallel execution and are marked independently, in any order.\n7. **Order steps** so that all dependencies of a step appear before that step.\n8. **Each step is one or two sentences of outcome.** State what the step achieves, cite the requirement(s) it serves, name the existing files or modules it builds on, and append the `\u2192 skill:` that supplies the syntax.\n9. **Create/Update a test-runner step.** Every pipeline plan carries a step that creates or\n   updates `run-tests.sh` which is the entry point for the project's tests, and runs it.\n   (`\u2192 skill: /test-sqrl`).\n10. **Include a shared-base-config step.** A plan that creates a new project or catalog carries\n    a checklist step to write the `<project>-shared-package.json` base config plus thin\n    `<project>-<env>-package.json` overlays (`-test`, `-prod`, `-local`, `-dev`) (part of\n    `/configure-sqrl` and `/build-catalog`).\n    - **Give each pipeline project and sub-project a `-test`, a `-dev` and a `-prod` overlay.**\n      Leave out an environment only when the requirements say that the environment is not\n      needed, and record that in `## Assumptions`.\n    - **The `-dev` overlay** uses sources and sinks that run on a local machine, so the pipeline\n      compiles and runs without production credentials. A `-prod` config often needs credentials\n      or services that the container does not have.\n    - **Use `-dev` as the name of the local environment.** Add a `-local` overlay only when the\n      requirements ask for it. Two names for one environment make the implementation guess.\n11. **Add data quality and observability after building the pipeline.** Every pipeline plan must include one checklist step that covers the work listed in `## Data Quality & Observability`. Place this step after all tasks that build or change the pipeline, including sources, transformations, outputs, and API definitions. Use `[deps: ...]` to make it wait until those tasks are complete. In this step, inspect the completed pipeline and build the needed data quality tables and observability statistics tables. Place final testing and documentation after this step so they include the monitoring. If nothing new is needed, state that in the step and explain why.\n12. **Assess whether a UI is required.** Dashboards, charts or other user-facing views in the requirements call for UI steps even without an explicit UI request. When a UI is required, add steps annotated with `\u2192 skill: /implement-ui` for the required views and interactions and their setup documentation.\n\n### 7. Data-Catalog File Format (CRITICAL)\n\nWhen writing a checklist for `scenario: catalog`, **all catalog files use `.sqrl` extension**.\nDataSQRL data catalogs are NOT YAML/JSON/CSV catalogs \u2014 they are DataSQRL scripts.\n\n**Never use**: `dataset.yaml`, `dataset.json`, `schema.yaml`, `catalog/*.yaml`, `.csv` test data.\n**Always use**: `.sqrl` for all scripts, `.jsonl` for all test data, `.json` for package files.\n\n**See the file layout from the `/build-catalog` skill and choosing the layout.** Layout selection part of the `/build-catalog` skill gives the default layout for a new catalog, the rule that an existing catalog keeps its own unless specified, and the rule that a reorganization request makes the default layout the target if desired organization is not expressed. Read the catalog before writing the plan, and move existing files only when the user asks for a reorganization.\n\n**State the governing layout in `## Technical Decisions`** and give every checklist step a full file path that matches it.\n\nAlways inspect `/opt/datasqrl-examples/finance-catalog/` as the canonical reference before writing\nany catalog checklist steps.\n\nFor `scenario: catalog`, `/build-catalog` is the primary skill, and the compile/test checklist\nstep references `ontology-test-package.json`, never a pipeline package.\n\n### 8. Skill-to-Task Guidance\n\nUse this table as a starting point, and include a skill in the plan only when a concrete step requires it \u2014 never speculatively. Read the skill in full before you assign a skill to a checklist item, so the step's text matches what that skill actually requires.\n\n| Skill | new-project | extend-project | connector-setup | catalog | architecture-design |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `/build-catalog` | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2713 always | \u2014 |\n| `/init-sqrl` | \u2713 always | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2713 if new project |\n| `/implement-sqrl` | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 if scripts need updating | \u2014 | \u2713 always |\n| `/configure-sqrl` | \u2713 always | \u2713 if config changes | \u2713 always | \u2014 | \u2713 always |\n| `/design-api` | \u2713 always | \u2713 if API changes | \u2713 if API affected | \u2014 | \u2713 always |\n| `/from-api` | \u2713 if API spec given | \u2713 if API spec given | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2713 if API spec given |\n| `/manage-connector` | \u2713 always | \u2713 if connectors change | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always |\n| `/data-observation` | \u2713 if data files provided | \u2713 if new data files | \u2713 if source data provided | \u2713 if test data from files | \u2713 if data files provided |\n| `/handle-large-data-files` | \u2713 if large data files | \u2713 if large data files | \u2713 if large data files | \u2713 if large data files | \u2713 if large data files |\n| `/implement-udf` | \u2713 if custom logic | \u2713 if custom logic | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2713 if custom logic |\n| `/implement-ui` | \u2713 if user-facing behavior requires UI | \u2713 if UI behavior is added or changed | \u2713 if UI is affected | \u2713 if UI is in scope | \u2713 if user-facing behavior requires UI |\n| `/test-sqrl` | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always |\n| `/troubleshoot-test` | \u2713 if tests fail | \u2713 if tests fail | \u2713 if tests fail | \u2713 if tests fail | \u2713 if tests fail |\n| `/write-documentation` | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always | \u2713 always |\n\n**Note on `/troubleshoot-test`**: this is a reactive skill. Include it as a conditional step\n(\"If tests fail after step N, use /troubleshoot-test\") rather than a mandatory step.\n\n**Note on `/from-api`**: include it whenever the requirements themselves specify the API the project must expose (GraphQL schema, REST/OpenAPI endpoints, or MCP tools). Annotate those steps with `\u2192 skill: /from-api` and order the checklist backwards from the contract: the API schema step comes before the pipeline-logic and connector steps, which depend on it.\n\n**Note on `/implement-ui`**: apply the UI assessment in Checklist Rule 12. Deferring visual design details does not make a dashboard deliverable API-only.\n\n**Note on `/data-observation` and `/handle-large-data-files`**: include both whenever the task provides source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling schemas or test data.\n\n### 9. Critical Rules\n\n- Never reference a file outside the root project.\n- Reference the requirements file for payloads, schemas, API contracts, test records and acceptance criteria.\n- Leave the names of tables, columns and functions the implementation will create to the implementation.\n- Never write SQRL, JSON, or Java statements in `## Implementation Checklist`,\n  `## Technical Decisions` or `## Data Quality & Observability`.\n- Never write a fenced code block in `## Implementation Checklist`.\n- Resolve every open question with a default in `## Assumptions`, tagged `[impact: ...]`.\n- Leave every checklist box unchecked: each one is written `- [ ]`.\n- Write only the single plan file; every other file stays untouched.\n\n### 10. Response\n\nRespond with exactly one line: the absolute path of the file you wrote.\nNothing else \u2014 no explanation, no summary.\n\n\n## Other projects in this repository (read-only)\n\nThis repository contains 3 other SQRL director(ies) besides your project. They are mounted **READ-ONLY**: you can read them for reference, but you cannot and must not modify them, and you must never copy their files into your project.\n\nA full inventory \u2014 paths, READMEs, SQRL file lists and package summaries \u2014 is in `.datasqrl/repo-inventory.md`. **Read it before planning or implementing.**\n\n**Shared module(s) \u2014 investigate these FIRST:**\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 declared under script.include by customer\n\n**Other project(s) \u2014 read after the shared module(s):**\n- `customer` (project)\n- `lending` (project)\n\nFor each: read its `README.md` first if it has one, then its `.sqrl` files, then its `package.json`. **Never read their `build/` directories** \u2014 that is compiler output and is deliberately excluded from the inventory.\n\n## Shared modules (read before configuring imports)\n\nThis project consumes external SQRL module(s) that are mounted as **read-only sibling directories** next to your project \u2014 NOT inside it. They are shared with other projects; you must **never create, copy, edit, or delete** any file under them. Treat them exactly like a shared data catalog.\n\nDeclare the module(s) you actually need under the `script.include` block in your project's `*-package.json` config files \u2014 in each config that carries a `script` block (the ones defining `script.main`, i.e. the configs you compile/test with); overlay configs that only define overwrites settings need none. Each include key is the **namespace** you import the module under (prefer underscores for each namespace); its `package` is the path, relative to your project root, to a package file at the module's top level \u2014 DataSQRL takes that file's folder as the include root and loads every `.sqrl` in it and its subfolders. Import their scripts by that namespace. Do **not** import them with a bare relative path and do **not** duplicate their contents into your project.\n\nAvailable shared module(s) \u2014 each shown with ONE of its top-level package files as the `package` example; any other package file at that module's top level works (the compiler only uses the file's folder as the include root):\n- `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\n\nExample package config `script` block:\n```json\n\"script\": {\n  \"main\": \"<your-main>.sqrl\",\n  \"include\": {\n      \"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }\n  },\n  \"config\": { \"variant\": \"test\" }\n}\n```\n\nImport a script from a module by its namespace (`{{variant}}` is resolved from `script.config`, so the same import serves test/prod):\n```sqrl\nIMPORT data_catalog.<script>-{{variant}} AS my_alias;\n```\n\nIf a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object, e.g. `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\", \"config\": { \"environment\": \"test\" } }` \u2014 otherwise unresolved placeholders become SQL parse errors.\nFrom a script in a subfolder of your project, use the `root` prefix: `IMPORT root.data_catalog.<script>;`.\n\nCompile/test from the project \u2014 the package path is resolved at the mount root, so it must be project-qualified:\n```bash\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh compile loan-payments/<your>-package.json\n/opt/agent/cmd.sh test loan-payments/<your>-test-package.json\n```"}], "timestamp": 1795509895426}}
