       |\n138:| `issuers[].algorithms`    | No       | `auth.algorithms`                | Per-issuer allowed algorithms                                                    |\n140:With neither `keys` nor `jwksUri`, keys come from OIDC discovery at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so\n141:`issuer` must then be an absolute http(s) URL. Keys are fetched once at startup; the server fails to start if they cannot\n144:any other algorithm are rejected. Set `algorithms` to restrict this, or to use keys without `alg` for other algorithms:\n146:example `\"algorithms\": [\"PS256\"]` for an RSA key without `alg` that signs `PS256`). Compilation fails if none of an issuer's inline\n147:`keys` can verify signatures, because it would accept unsigned tokens; for keys from a `jwksUri` or discovery, the server\n148:does not start. Unknown keys, duplicate issuers, unsupported algorithms and the other invalid\n151:The MCP discovery endpoint `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` advertises the issuers without inline `keys` whose\n152:`issuer` is a URL as `authorization_servers`. Issuers with inline `keys` sign their own tokens and are not advertised;\n153:when no issuer is advertised, the endpoint is not served and MCP `401` responses carry no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n155:For a shared secret (tests), use inline `\"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"<base64url secret>\" }]`; the `/design-api` skill's\n156:[authorization.md](../design-api/authorization.md#generating-tokens) shows how to encode it.\n158:String values can reference environment variables, e.g. `\"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\"`. They are resolved when the\n161:Package layers merge objects key by key but replace arrays whole: a later layer can patch `auth.leeway` alone, but a\n162:layer that sets `auth.issuers` must list every issuer of its environment. `auth` cannot be combined with `jwtAuth` or\n163:`oauthConfig`, even across layers; set the old block to `null` in the layer that introduces `auth`.\n165:## JWT Authentication (deprecated)\n167:`jwtAuth` is deprecated; use `auth`. Its field names map to Vert.x Java POJOs and are case-sensitive. Compilation fails if it has no key that can verify signatures (for example with no `pubSecKeys`, `jwks` or `keyStore`). If none of the keys `oauthConfig` discovers can verify signatures, the server does not start.\n171:  \"engines\": {\n180:            \"issuer\": \"https://issuer.example\",\n181:            \"audience\": [\"my-api\"],\n194:## OAuth 2.0 Authentication (deprecated)\n196:`oauthConfig` is deprecated because it checks neither `iss` nor `aud`; use `auth`. OAuth enables MCP clients to discover the authorization server at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`.\n200:  \"engines\": {\n===FIND TESTDATA===\ntotal 12244\ndrwxr-xr-x 17 root root     544 Nov 24 08:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 13 root root     416 Nov 24 08:27 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  157145 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-auto_loan.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   38474 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-consumer_loan_delinquency_event.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  312922 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-loan_application.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1149330 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  142600 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   45087 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-card_delinquency_event.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  229821 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_account.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  188425 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1040770 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_payment.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1511049 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_statement.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  206287 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_limit_history.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1236995 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-credit_utilization.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 3174132 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-customer_credit_exposure.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  378776 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-delinquency_rollforward.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 2689373 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-payment_behavior.jsonl\n===HEAD PERSONAL===\n{\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-023\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-066\",\"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-28229\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"MAJOR_PURCHASE\",\"original_amount_cents\":1740000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1566,\"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\"monthly_payment_cents\":84913,\"funding_date\":\"2020-09-11\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-10-11\",\"maturity_date\":\"2022-09-11\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-09-04\",\"origination_fee_cents\":34800,\"credit_score_at_origination\":644,\"dti_at_origination\":24.62,\"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"origination_channel\":\"BRANCH\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:40:57Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:44:22Z\"}\n{\"loan_id\":\"PL-014\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-026\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-392\",\"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-51718\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"HOME_IMPROVEMENT\",\"original_amount_cents\":910000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1032,\"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\"monthly_payment_cents\":42126,\"funding_date\":\"2020-11-08\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-12-08\",\"maturity_date\":\"2022-11-08\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-10-26\",\"origination_fee_cents\":18200,\"credit_score_at_origination\":733,\"dti_at_origination\":28.97,\"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-450\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-450\",\"origination_channel\":\"ONLINE\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2022-10-26T14:59:04Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-10-26T15:01:21Z\"}\n===WC===\n    135 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-auto_loan.jsonl\n     56 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-consumer_loan_delinquency_event.jsonl\n    382 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-loan_application.jsonl\n   1942 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\n    170 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\n   2685 total\n"}], "details": {}}}
{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:45:11.023153+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cbafbe876a3b27a07bb", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "5:| `authKind` | **array**  | `[]`      | Not read by the server; `auth` needs no `authKind`    |\n6:| `config`   | **object** | see below | Vert.x-specific configuration including auth settings |\n10:The compiler generates the full server configuration from a built-in template and deep-merges `engines.vertx.config` over it, so any server setting can be overridden, not just authentication. Nested objects are merged key by key, while arrays replace the default array. Commonly overridden settings:\n25:  \"engines\": {\n41:For all settings and their effective values, compile the project and read the generated `deploy/plan/vertx-config.json` under the build directory (`build/` normally or `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`). It contains the full server configuration with the overrides from `engines.vertx.config` applied.\n51:  \"engines\": {\n54:        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n64:All keys go under `engines.vertx.config.graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig`:\n90:All keys go under `engines.vertx.config.jdbcStatementLogConfig.largeResults`. `jdbcStatementLogConfig` holds the logs about individual database queries, and each has its own `enabled` flag:\n97:## Authentication\n99:Configure `auth` with the list of trusted token issuers. Each issuer carries its own audiences and signing keys, and a\n100:token is accepted only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature all match the same issuer.\n104:  \"engines\": {\n107:        \"auth\": {\n108:          \"issuers\": [\n110:              \"issuer\": \"https://login.example.com/\",\n111:              \"audiences\": [\"https://app.example.com\", \"https://admin.example.com\"]\n114:              \"issuer\": \"https://<tenant>.us.auth0.com/\",\n115:              \"audiences\": [\"https://api.example.com/\"]\n128:| `issuers`                 | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty array of trusted issuers                                               |\n129:| `leeway`                  | No       | `60`                             | Clock skew in seconds; resolution order is `issuers[].leeway`, `leeway`, 60      |\n130:| `algorithms`              | No       | no restriction                   | Allowed signature algorithms; an issuer's own `algorithms` overrides it          |\n133:| `issuers[].issuer`        | Yes      |                                  | Exact `iss` value, including a trailing slash; unique                            |\n134:| `issuers[].audiences`     | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty; the token's `aud` must contain one of them                            |\n135:| `issuers[].keys`          | No       |                                  | Inline JWKs (RFC 7517); cannot rotate, so prefer discovery                       |\n136:| `issuers[].jwksUri`       | No       |                                  | JWK Set URL; set at most one of `keys` and `jwksUri`                             |\n137:| `issuers[].leeway`        | No       | `auth.leeway`                    | Per-issuer leeway; negative values are rejected                                  |\n138:| `issuers[].algorithms`    | No       | `auth.algorithms`                | Per-issuer allowed algorithms                                                    |\n140:With neither `keys` nor `jwksUri`, keys come from OIDC discovery at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so\n141:`issuer` must then be an absolute http(s) URL. Keys are fetched once at startup; the server fails to start if they cannot\n144:any other algorithm are rejected. Set `algorithms` to restrict this, or to use keys without `alg` for other algorithms:\n146:example `\"algorithms\": [\"PS256\"]` for an RSA key without `alg` that signs `PS256`). Compilation fails if none of an issuer's inline\n147:`keys` can verify signatures, because it would accept unsigned tokens; for keys from a `jwksUri` or discovery, the server\n148:does not start. Unknown keys, duplicate issuers, unsupported algorithms and the other invalid\n151:The MCP discovery endpoint `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` advertises the issuers without inline `keys` whose\n152:`issuer` is a URL as `authorization_servers`. Issuers with inline `keys` sign their own tokens and are not advertised;\n153:when no issuer is advertised, the endpoint is not served and MCP `401` responses carry no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n155:For a shared secret (tests), use inline `\"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"<base64url secret>\" }]`; the `/design-api` skill's\n156:[authorization.md](../design-api/authorization.md#generating-tokens) shows how to encode it.\n158:String values can reference environment variables, e.g. `\"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\"`. They are resolved when the\n161:Package layers merge objects key by key but replace arrays whole: a later layer can patch `auth.leeway` alone, but a\n162:layer that sets `auth.issuers` must list every issuer of its environment. `auth` cannot be combined with `jwtAuth` or\n163:`oauthConfig`, even across layers; set the old block to `null` in the layer that introduces `auth`.\n165:## JWT Authentication (deprecated)\n167:`jwtAuth` is deprecated; use `auth`. Its field names map to Vert.x Java POJOs and are case-sensitive. Compilation fails if it has no key that can verify signatures (for example with no `pubSecKeys`, `jwks` or `keyStore`). If none of the keys `oauthConfig` discovers can verify signatures, the server does not start.\n171:  \"engines\": {\n180:            \"issuer\": \"https://issuer.example\",\n181:            \"audience\": [\"my-api\"],\n194:## OAuth 2.0 Authentication (deprecated)\n196:`oauthConfig` is deprecated because it checks neither `iss` nor `aud`; use `auth`. OAuth enables MCP clients to discover the authorization server at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`.\n200:  \"engines\": {\n===FIND TESTDATA===\ntotal 12244\ndrwxr-xr-x 17 root root     544 Nov 24 08:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 13 root root     416 Nov 24 08:27 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  157145 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-auto_loan.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   38474 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-consumer_loan_delinquency_event.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  312922 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-loan_application.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1149330 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  142600 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   45087 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-card_delinquency_event.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  229821 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_account.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  188425 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1040770 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_payment.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1511049 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_statement.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  206287 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_limit_history.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1236995 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-credit_utilization.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 3174132 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-customer_credit_exposure.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  378776 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-delinquency_rollforward.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 2689373 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-payment_behavior.jsonl\n===HEAD PERSONAL===\n{\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-023\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-066\",\"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-28229\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"MAJOR_PURCHASE\",\"original_amount_cents\":1740000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1566,\"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\"monthly_payment_cents\":84913,\"funding_date\":\"2020-09-11\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-10-11\",\"maturity_date\":\"2022-09-11\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-09-04\",\"origination_fee_cents\":34800,\"credit_score_at_origination\":644,\"dti_at_origination\":24.62,\"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"origination_channel\":\"BRANCH\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:40:57Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:44:22Z\"}\n{\"loan_id\":\"PL-014\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-026\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-392\",\"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-51718\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"HOME_IMPROVEMENT\",\"original_amount_cents\":910000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1032,\"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\"monthly_payment_cents\":42126,\"funding_date\":\"2020-11-08\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-12-08\",\"maturity_date\":\"2022-11-08\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-10-26\",\"origination_fee_cents\":18200,\"credit_score_at_origination\":733,\"dti_at_origination\":28.97,\"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-450\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-450\",\"origination_channel\":\"ONLINE\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2022-10-26T14:59:04Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-10-26T15:01:21Z\"}\n===WC===\n    135 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-auto_loan.jsonl\n     56 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-consumer_loan_delinquency_event.jsonl\n    382 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-loan_application.jsonl\n   1942 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\n    170 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\n   2685 total\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 23, "timestamp": 1795509911022}}
{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:45:11.023510+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cbafcfc7e11a38f114d", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: init-sqrl\ndescription: Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\nmodes: [planning, implementation, setup, question]\n---\n\nFollow these steps to initialize a DataSQRL project:\n1. When an available examples directory contains a useful starting point, review its `README.md` and relevant projects. Treat its contents as optional reference material, not required templates.\n2. Adapt relevant example files into the working directory only when they fit the task. Don't overwrite existing files. If no example fits, scaffold the project with `/opt/agent/cmd.sh init <type> <name>` instead (it creates the `<name>/` folder; see `CLI_REFERENCE.md`). Pick the type by the pipeline's output:\n   * `api`: data served through a GraphQL API (Flink, PostgreSQL, Kafka, Vert.x). The production source `connectors/sources-prod.sqrl` is an internal Kafka table (`/*+ engine(kafka) */`), exposed as a mutation.\n   * `stream`: results written to Kafka topics (Flink, Kafka). The production source is an internal Kafka table, and the script exports its result with `EXPORT HelloWorld TO log.HelloWorld;`. Without an API, only exported tables are written anywhere: export each result the user needs, and the script must have at least one `EXPORT` to compile.\n   * `dataset`: results written to Iceberg and queried through DuckDB (Flink, Iceberg, DuckDB). The production source is an existing Iceberg table (`'connector' = 'iceberg'`): set its catalog options (`catalog-type`, `catalog-name`, `warehouse`, `catalog-table`) to the user's lakehouse.\n   * The test package of every type reads the sample data in `connectors/test-data/` and compiles and tests out of the box. Replace the `Messages` sources and the sample data with the real sources (step 4).\n3. Review provided source/sink table definitions in sub-folders to identify the sqrl files that define the sources and sinks required for the implementation. If the requirements reference a provided data catalog, include that catalog and delete copied connectors unless the requirements specifically ask to create additional sources/sinks outside the catalog.\n4. Update copied files to match user requirements by:\n   * **ALWAYS** invoke the relevant skill before updating or implementing anything\n   * Use user-provided source/sink definitions from sub-folders or an included data catalog if they exist (include/import them, reusing what exists), otherwise update the template connectors. Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill (Connector Organization) before writing or editing any source/sink definition.\n   * Updating the SQRL files to use those sources & sinks and adjust implementation by matching the code to the updated table schemas and removing features that are not required. Invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill before writing or significantly modifying any SQRL logic during this step.\n   * When renaming the main `.sqrl` script, update `script.main` key in the (sub-)project's base config so every package.json reference matches the actual filename before the first compile. When creating or updating any configuration, invoke `/configure-sqrl` skill.\n   * Update the project configuration if necessary.\n   * Update the GraphQL API definition and defined operations to match the new schema.\n   * Create or update the configuration files: a `*-shared-package.json` base plus `*-<env>-package.json` thin per-environment overlays. Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Base Config and Environment Overlays).\n   * Add `/*+test */` annotated SQL test queries at the end of the main SQRL script(s) for each exposed table. These test queries should SELECT from the table with a WHERE filter on a known test data value and produce deterministic results. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill (Writing Tests) for the test and test-data rules, then delete the template's existing snapshots and run the tests to generate fresh ones (Running Tests, Snapshot Lifecycle).\n   * Create or update the test runner script `run-tests.sh` which is the entry point for the project's tests. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for details.\n   * Update `README.md` with user requirements and project description. Add a list of features that are required by the user but not part of the template and need to be implemented\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 8, "timestamp": 1795509911022}}
{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:45:11.023812+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now let me read the vertx auth reference and init-sqrl briefly, and examine test data. Let me also check the requirements file to be sure the prompt matches. Actually the prompt already contains the full requirements. But the file must be referenced.\n\nLet me read the vertx auth section. And check the requirements file exists. Let me also glance at the test data to understand structure (data-observation). Given plan only, I don't need to compute expected values (the planner computes? Actually the plan says \"The planner computes the expected values from the test data.\" Hmm, but the plan just references requirements; downstream implementation computes. I'll leave it.\n\nLet me read the vertx.md auth and check test data files.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cbafbe876a3b27a07bb", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "sed -n '1,200p' /opt/agent/skills/configure-sqrl/vertx.md | grep -n \"auth\\|Authentication\\|issuer\\|audience\\|keys\\|engine\\|graphQLTail\" | head -60; echo \"===FIND TESTDATA===\"; ls -la /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/ 2>/dev/null; echo \"===HEAD PERSONAL===\"; head -2 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl; echo \"===WC===\"; wc -l /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-*.jsonl"}}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cbafcfc7e11a38f114d", "name": "read", "arguments": {"path": "/opt/agent/skills/init-sqrl/SKILL.md"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 13192, "output": 393, "cacheRead": 65024, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 149, "totalTokens": 78609, "cost": {"input": 0.0039575999999999995, "output": 0.00047159999999999997, "cacheRead": 0.000390144, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.004819344}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795509908814, "responseId": "130806eb03834e77b5b4af1cca3fc0a1-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 2183, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, "toolResults": [{"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cbafbe876a3b27a07bb", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "5:| `authKind` | **array**  | `[]`      | Not read by the server; `auth` needs no `authKind`    |\n6:| `config`   | **object** | see below | Vert.x-specific configuration including auth settings |\n10:The compiler generates the full server configuration from a built-in template and deep-merges `engines.vertx.config` over it, so any server setting can be overridden, not just authentication. Nested objects are merged key by key, while arrays replace the default array. Commonly overridden settings:\n25:  \"engines\": {\n41:For all settings and their effective values, compile the project and read the generated `deploy/plan/vertx-config.json` under the build directory (`build/` normally or `build/<sub-project>/` when compiling with `-b`). It contains the full server configuration with the overrides from `engines.vertx.config` applied.\n51:  \"engines\": {\n54:        \"graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig\": {\n64:All keys go under `engines.vertx.config.graphQLTailSampleTracingConfig`:\n90:All keys go under `engines.vertx.config.jdbcStatementLogConfig.largeResults`. `jdbcStatementLogConfig` holds the logs about individual database queries, and each has its own `enabled` flag:\n97:## Authentication\n99:Configure `auth` with the list of trusted token issuers. Each issuer carries its own audiences and signing keys, and a\n100:token is accepted only when its `iss`, `aud` and signature all match the same issuer.\n104:  \"engines\": {\n107:        \"auth\": {\n108:          \"issuers\": [\n110:              \"issuer\": \"https://login.example.com/\",\n111:              \"audiences\": [\"https://app.example.com\", \"https://admin.example.com\"]\n114:              \"issuer\": \"https://<tenant>.us.auth0.com/\",\n115:              \"audiences\": [\"https://api.example.com/\"]\n128:| `issuers`                 | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty array of trusted issuers                                               |\n129:| `leeway`                  | No       | `60`                             | Clock skew in seconds; resolution order is `issuers[].leeway`, `leeway`, 60      |\n130:| `algorithms`              | No       | no restriction                   | Allowed signature algorithms; an issuer's own `algorithms` overrides it          |\n133:| `issuers[].issuer`        | Yes      |                                  | Exact `iss` value, including a trailing slash; unique                            |\n134:| `issuers[].audiences`     | Yes      |                                  | Non-empty; the token's `aud` must contain one of them                            |\n135:| `issuers[].keys`          | No       |                                  | Inline JWKs (RFC 7517); cannot rotate, so prefer discovery                       |\n136:| `issuers[].jwksUri`       | No       |                                  | JWK Set URL; set at most one of `keys` and `jwksUri`                             |\n137:| `issuers[].leeway`        | No       | `auth.leeway`                    | Per-issuer leeway; negative values are rejected                                  |\n138:| `issuers[].algorithms`    | No       | `auth.algorithms`                | Per-issuer allowed algorithms                                                    |\n140:With neither `keys` nor `jwksUri`, keys come from OIDC discovery at `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`, so\n141:`issuer` must then be an absolute http(s) URL. Keys are fetched once at startup; the server fails to start if they cannot\n144:any other algorithm are rejected. Set `algorithms` to restrict this, or to use keys without `alg` for other algorithms:\n146:example `\"algorithms\": [\"PS256\"]` for an RSA key without `alg` that signs `PS256`). Compilation fails if none of an issuer's inline\n147:`keys` can verify signatures, because it would accept unsigned tokens; for keys from a `jwksUri` or discovery, the server\n148:does not start. Unknown keys, duplicate issuers, unsupported algorithms and the other invalid\n151:The MCP discovery endpoint `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` advertises the issuers without inline `keys` whose\n152:`issuer` is a URL as `authorization_servers`. Issuers with inline `keys` sign their own tokens and are not advertised;\n153:when no issuer is advertised, the endpoint is not served and MCP `401` responses carry no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n155:For a shared secret (tests), use inline `\"keys\": [{ \"kty\": \"oct\", \"k\": \"<base64url secret>\" }]`; the `/design-api` skill's\n156:[authorization.md](../design-api/authorization.md#generating-tokens) shows how to encode it.\n158:String values can reference environment variables, e.g. `\"issuer\": \"${AUTH_ISSUER}\"`. They are resolved when the\n161:Package layers merge objects key by key but replace arrays whole: a later layer can patch `auth.leeway` alone, but a\n162:layer that sets `auth.issuers` must list every issuer of its environment. `auth` cannot be combined with `jwtAuth` or\n163:`oauthConfig`, even across layers; set the old block to `null` in the layer that introduces `auth`.\n165:## JWT Authentication (deprecated)\n167:`jwtAuth` is deprecated; use `auth`. Its field names map to Vert.x Java POJOs and are case-sensitive. Compilation fails if it has no key that can verify signatures (for example with no `pubSecKeys`, `jwks` or `keyStore`). If none of the keys `oauthConfig` discovers can verify signatures, the server does not start.\n171:  \"engines\": {\n180:            \"issuer\": \"https://issuer.example\",\n181:            \"audience\": [\"my-api\"],\n194:## OAuth 2.0 Authentication (deprecated)\n196:`oauthConfig` is deprecated because it checks neither `iss` nor `aud`; use `auth`. OAuth enables MCP clients to discover the authorization server at `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`.\n200:  \"engines\": {\n===FIND TESTDATA===\ntotal 12244\ndrwxr-xr-x 17 root root     544 Nov 24 08:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 13 root root     416 Nov 24 08:27 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  157145 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-auto_loan.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   38474 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-consumer_loan_delinquency_event.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  312922 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-loan_application.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1149330 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  142600 Nov 24 08:27 consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root   45087 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-card_delinquency_event.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  229821 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_account.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  188425 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1040770 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_payment.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1511049 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_card_statement.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  206287 Nov 24 08:27 credit_cards-credit_limit_history.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 1236995 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-credit_utilization.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 3174132 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-customer_credit_exposure.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root  378776 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-delinquency_rollforward.jsonl\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 2689373 Nov 24 08:27 credit_risk_signals-payment_behavior.jsonl\n===HEAD PERSONAL===\n{\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-023\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-066\",\"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-28229\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"MAJOR_PURCHASE\",\"original_amount_cents\":1740000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1566,\"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\"monthly_payment_cents\":84913,\"funding_date\":\"2020-09-11\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-10-11\",\"maturity_date\":\"2022-09-11\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-09-04\",\"origination_fee_cents\":34800,\"credit_score_at_origination\":644,\"dti_at_origination\":24.62,\"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-140\",\"origination_channel\":\"BRANCH\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:40:57Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-09-04T12:44:22Z\"}\n{\"loan_id\":\"PL-014\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-026\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-392\",\"loan_number\":\"PL-2020-51718\",\"loan_status\":\"PAID_OFF\",\"loan_purpose\":\"HOME_IMPROVEMENT\",\"original_amount_cents\":910000,\"current_balance_cents\":0,\"interest_rate\":0.1032,\"rate_type\":\"FIXED\",\"original_term_months\":24,\"remaining_term_months\":0,\"monthly_payment_cents\":42126,\"funding_date\":\"2020-11-08\",\"first_payment_date\":\"2020-12-08\",\"maturity_date\":\"2022-11-08\",\"next_payment_due_date\":null,\"paid_off_date\":\"2022-10-26\",\"origination_fee_cents\":18200,\"credit_score_at_origination\":733,\"dti_at_origination\":28.97,\"autopay_enrolled\":true,\"disbursement_account_id\":\"ACCT-450\",\"payment_account_id\":\"ACCT-450\",\"origination_channel\":\"ONLINE\",\"source_system\":\"CONSUMER_LENDING\",\"source_updated_at\":\"2022-10-26T14:59:04Z\",\"ingested_at\":\"2022-10-26T15:01:21Z\"}\n===WC===\n    135 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-auto_loan.jsonl\n     56 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-consumer_loan_delinquency_event.jsonl\n    382 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-loan_application.jsonl\n   1942 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl\n    170 /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata/consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl\n   2685 total\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 23, "timestamp": 1795509911022}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cbafcfc7e11a38f114d", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "---\nname: init-sqrl\ndescription: Use when starting a new DataSQRL project or initializing an empty pipeline. Use for greenfield implementations, project scaffolding, or setting up the folder structure.\nmodes: [planning, implementation, setup, question]\n---\n\nFollow these steps to initialize a DataSQRL project:\n1. When an available examples directory contains a useful starting point, review its `README.md` and relevant projects. Treat its contents as optional reference material, not required templates.\n2. Adapt relevant example files into the working directory only when they fit the task. Don't overwrite existing files. If no example fits, scaffold the project with `/opt/agent/cmd.sh init <type> <name>` instead (it creates the `<name>/` folder; see `CLI_REFERENCE.md`). Pick the type by the pipeline's output:\n   * `api`: data served through a GraphQL API (Flink, PostgreSQL, Kafka, Vert.x). The production source `connectors/sources-prod.sqrl` is an internal Kafka table (`/*+ engine(kafka) */`), exposed as a mutation.\n   * `stream`: results written to Kafka topics (Flink, Kafka). The production source is an internal Kafka table, and the script exports its result with `EXPORT HelloWorld TO log.HelloWorld;`. Without an API, only exported tables are written anywhere: export each result the user needs, and the script must have at least one `EXPORT` to compile.\n   * `dataset`: results written to Iceberg and queried through DuckDB (Flink, Iceberg, DuckDB). The production source is an existing Iceberg table (`'connector' = 'iceberg'`): set its catalog options (`catalog-type`, `catalog-name`, `warehouse`, `catalog-table`) to the user's lakehouse.\n   * The test package of every type reads the sample data in `connectors/test-data/` and compiles and tests out of the box. Replace the `Messages` sources and the sample data with the real sources (step 4).\n3. Review provided source/sink table definitions in sub-folders to identify the sqrl files that define the sources and sinks required for the implementation. If the requirements reference a provided data catalog, include that catalog and delete copied connectors unless the requirements specifically ask to create additional sources/sinks outside the catalog.\n4. Update copied files to match user requirements by:\n   * **ALWAYS** invoke the relevant skill before updating or implementing anything\n   * Use user-provided source/sink definitions from sub-folders or an included data catalog if they exist (include/import them, reusing what exists), otherwise update the template connectors. Invoke the `/manage-connector` skill (Connector Organization) before writing or editing any source/sink definition.\n   * Updating the SQRL files to use those sources & sinks and adjust implementation by matching the code to the updated table schemas and removing features that are not required. Invoke the `/implement-sqrl` skill before writing or significantly modifying any SQRL logic during this step.\n   * When renaming the main `.sqrl` script, update `script.main` key in the (sub-)project's base config so every package.json reference matches the actual filename before the first compile. When creating or updating any configuration, invoke `/configure-sqrl` skill.\n   * Update the project configuration if necessary.\n   * Update the GraphQL API definition and defined operations to match the new schema.\n   * Create or update the configuration files: a `*-shared-package.json` base plus `*-<env>-package.json` thin per-environment overlays. Invoke the `/configure-sqrl` skill (Base Config and Environment Overlays).\n   * Add `/*+test */` annotated SQL test queries at the end of the main SQRL script(s) for each exposed table. These test queries should SELECT from the table with a WHERE filter on a known test data value and produce deterministic results. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill (Writing Tests) for the test and test-data rules, then delete the template's existing snapshots and run the tests to generate fresh ones (Running Tests, Snapshot Lifecycle).\n   * Create or update the test runner script `run-tests.sh` which is the entry point for the project's tests. Invoke the `/test-sqrl` skill for details.\n   * Update `README.md` with user requirements and project description. Add a list of features that are required by the user but not part of the template and need to be implemented\n"}], "isError": false, "durationMs": 8, "timestamp": 1795509911022}]}
