{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:45:46.201811+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "turn_end", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Good. The referential integrity is asserted. So the inner join drops nothing by the guarantee. No DQ needed for the join.\n\nWhat about FR-8 (status change)? No test data. Coverage by design. Not DQ.\n\nObservability: count of personal payments received vs dropped by loan_type filter. The auto-loan payments are intentionally excluded (932 rows). Not a problem. So no OB needed? The requirements don't ask for monitoring. The plan must include a DQ/Observability checklist step (Rule 11), even if nothing new needed, stating why. I'll make one step that inspects the pipeline and concludes nothing new is needed, because the catalog asserts referential integrity and exclusions are intentional; but I could add one useful table: OB counts of payments by loan_type to detect catalog changes? Hmm, rule says only if a blind spot matters and someone acts. I'll lean to \"Skip\" with rationale, and mention the catalog's existing assertions/root snapshots. Perhaps one DQ for payments with a matching loan but loan_type mismatch? Not credible.\n\nActually, there might be a real blind spot: the API authorization assumes the JWT `customer_id` claim exists. FR-13 rejects missing claim (NOT NULL arg \u2192 403). Covered by test.\n\nI'll say: Build \u2014 none needed; Skip \u2014 missing-loan join capture not needed because catalog asserts it; OB ingestion counts not needed because no independent senders and catalog snapshots already cover schema/content changes. Reuse \u2014 catalog's lending ontology assertions (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` at `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`) and root ontology snapshot queries. Also mention the sibling projects don't carry DQ either.\n\nNow, regarding UI: no UI required (out of scope explicitly). So no UI steps.\n\nNow let me think about the plan checklist in detail. Single sub-project `loan-payments` pipeline. Steps:\n\n1. Scaffold the project structure & configs (init). `\u2192 skill: /init-sqrl`. Actually init-sqrl is for scaffolding. Since we're building a new project, use /init-sqrl. But the project is essentially modeled on lending. I'll do:\n   - Step 1: Initialize the project from the nearest reference (`lending/`) with the shared base and test/prod overlays, declaring the catalog under script.include. `\u2192 /init-sqrl, /configure-sqrl`. Wait, the rule says init-sqrl always for new-project.\n\nHmm. Let me structure:\n\n1. Scaffold project skeleton modeled on `lending/` (main sqrl, api dir, package configs). `\u2192 /init-sqrl`\n2. Create shared base + test/prod overlays, declaring catalog include and engines. `\u2192 /configure-sqrl`\n   - This includes the shared-base-config step (rule 10). Could combine 1 and 2. But rule 10 says include a shared-base-config step. I'll separate: step 1 scaffold, step 2 configs.\n3. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations from the requirements API surface (backwards derivation). `\u2192 /from-api, /design-api`. This comes before pipeline logic (order backwards from contract).\n4. Configure auth provider (vertx auth block) for test/prod. `\u2192 /configure-sqrl`. Could be part of step 2. But it's needed for compile of claim functions. I'll fold into step 2 or make separate. I'll make it separate step 4 maybe. Actually step 3 (schema) doesn't read claims until script does. Let me order:\n   1. scaffold\n   2. configs (base+overlays, include, engines) \n   3. API schema + operations\n   4. auth config\n   5. main SQRL: import catalog, dedup latest loans & payments, build LoanPayments\n   6. summary (FR-6/7 + zero-payment loans)\n   7. table functions/relationships for auth-filtered queries (the three lookups)\n   8. data quality/observability step\n   9. tests (API tests incl. auth) \n   10. run-tests.sh\n   11. README/documentation\n   plus conditional troubleshoot.\n\nLet me merge appropriately and order dependencies. Also the data quality step must be after all pipeline/API changes (rule 11) and before final testing. Hmm, rule 11 says \"Place final testing and documentation after this step.\" So order: ... pipeline steps ... DQ/Obs step ... tests ... run-tests ... docs.\n\nBut tests for DQ tables belong in tests. Fine.\n\nLet me also decide: do we need a `/manage-connector` step? The sources are imported from the catalog via include; no new connectors. So no. But rule says manage-connector if connectors change. They don't. Good. However, including the catalog and selecting test/prod connectors is via config, not writing connectors. So /configure-sqrl.\n\n`/data-observation`: The task provides test data files to inspect. We already inspected. Should the implementation agent inspect? The plan can reference test data. I could add a step to inspect catalog test data with /data-observation before modeling/verifying. But we already know its shape. Given rule, \"include both whenever the task provides source/sample/test data files to inspect before modeling schemas or writing test data.\" There are test data files. I'll add a small observation step early (step 1 maybe) or annotate the test step. I'll add an early step: inspect catalog test data (`consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl`, `consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl`) to confirm payload shapes and expected counts. `\u2192 /data-observation`. But it's catalog data, read-only, no large file issue (1MB). Actually 1.1MB is not large. So no /handle-large-data-files.\n\nDo we need /implement-udf? No.\n\nLet me now think about whether the schema should be custom at all. The requirements API surface: three queries with auth claims. Given auth claims and a customer query without customer_id argument, a custom schema plus table functions is warranted. Also requirements list exact table/type names. So /from-api applies (requirements partially define the API). Yes.\n\nNow, technical decisions:\n\n- **Custom GraphQL schema + operations** rather than inferred, because the API must hide caller-supplied `customer_id` (injected from JWT) and name types `LoanPayments`/`LoanPaymentSummary`. Follows lending's `lending360-api/`.\n- **Streaming Flink + Postgres + Vertx**, no Kafka engine (no mutations/subscriptions).\n- **Catalog include**: namespace `data_catalog`, package a top-level catalog file (`../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json`) with `is_batch:false`, exactly like lending; consumer `{{environment}}` selects the connector file. \n- **Dedup with DISTINCT by source_updated_at** (FR-2), non-windowed aggregation (FR-8 freshness/late payments).\n- **Summary spine = all latest personal loans LEFT JOIN applied-payment aggregates**, so loans without payments appear with zeros (Open Question default).\n- **Auth via table functions with METADATA FROM 'auth.customer_id'** and vertx auth block (HS256 test, `${JWT_SECRET}` prod).\n- **Unbounded state** for dedup and aggregation accepted; retention is all payments (non-functional). Note state growth.\n- No windowing (freshness 1 min via streaming).\n\nLet me check: is there a concern that `DISTINCT` + unbounded group by state grows unbounded? Requirements accept. Record in decisions/assumptions.\n\nAssumptions (from Open Questions + others):\n- Freshness: ~1 min, streaming Flink, no window. [high]\n- JWT validation: HS256 test secret, `${JWT_SECRET}` prod; issuer/audience placeholders. [high]\n- Production workload: up to 1M loans, 50k payments/day, 50 rps. [medium]\n- last_payment_date = latest applied_date of applied. [low]\n- total_paid_cents = sum payment_amount_cents of applied. [low]\n- Loans without payments: include with zeros. [medium]\n- Loan status: all statuses. [medium]\n- Customer query loan details: loan_status, original_amount_cents, current_balance_cents, monthly_payment_cents, next_payment_due_date. [low]\n- API protocols: GraphQL + REST + MCP. [low]\n- Budget: smallest DataSQRL Cloud deployment. [low]\n- Dev environment: requirements only ask test + prod; omit dev overlay. [low]\n- Data catalog include config pinned to test environment (like lending) while connectors are selected by consumer environment. [medium?] Actually the consumer selects test/prod connectors via `{{environment}}`; the included catalog's own scripts use environment test but aren't imported. I'll record.\n- FR-8 not covered by a snapshot because no status change in catalog data; covered by design. [low]\n- Engines: no kafka engine since no mutations/subscriptions. [medium]\n\nAlso maybe: single sub-project named `loan_payments`. [low]\n\nNow the frontmatter domains: finance. Maybe also analytics? No. I'll list `finance`.\n\nScenario: pipeline. Task: new-project.\n\nDate: Use a plausible current UTC matching timestamp. The plan file is `plan_20261124-084455.md`, requirements `requirements_20261124-113440.md` (future date 2026). Date: 2026-11-24T08:44:55Z.\n\nNow let me write the plan file. Follow the section-by-section method: skeleton Write, then one Edit per section.\n\nLet me draft the sections.\n\n### Requirements Source\n`adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`. Also reference catalog files and sibling `lending/` for layout; test data paths (read-only, outside project \u2014 hmm, \"Any referenced files must be in the project.\" The rule says referenced files must be in the project. The catalog files are outside the project (read-only shared module). But the requirements file itself references them. The plan can reference `adr/requirements_...md` and project files. Should I reference `data-catalog/...`? The rule \"Any referenced files must be in the project\" is strict. But the catalog is a shared module; referencing it is necessary. I'll reference the requirements file primarily and mention the catalog tables by name via requirements. For paths, maybe avoid external paths or mention them as the shared module (which the implementation agent must read anyway). Hmm. I'll reference the requirements file and the sibling lending layout by path (`../lending/...`)? That's outside project. To be safe, reference only `adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md` and project-relative paths. But the shared module is at `../data-catalog` and the requirements hard-constraint references it. I think it's acceptable to name the shared module and catalog tables as requirements do; the implementation agent can resolve. I'll keep external path mentions minimal but include the catalog include path since it's part of config and the requirements state it. Actually the requirements hard constraint says include via script.include \"the same way as lending/lending360-shared-package.json\". I'll mention the catalog by module name and top-level package path in Technical Decisions (configuration-related, unavoidable). The \"files in the project\" rule likely aims at not referencing arbitrary outside files; the shared module is explicitly expected. I'll proceed.\n\n### Implementation Checklist\nLet me write steps with deps and skills.\n\n- [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog test data for personal loans and loan payments to confirm field shapes, value ranges, and the edge cases the acceptance criteria depend on. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n   (This is reading catalog test data, not in project. Hmm, but it's fine.)\n- [ ] 2. Scaffold the project from the sibling `lending/` layout: main script, API folder, snapshots/test folders, README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- [ ] 3. Write the base config and the `test`/`prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the engine set, API version, and test-runner; set the consumer `environment` per overlay so the catalog selects the test/prod connectors. [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, matching table/type names and hiding the JWT-injected `customer_id`. [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n- [ ] 5. Configure the vertx auth provider in the test and prod overlays so claim-reading functions compile. [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 6. Implement the main pipeline: import the catalog consumer-loans scripts, dedup latest loans and payments by `source_updated_at`, keep personal payments, and build `LoanPayments` with customer/loan fields (FR-1\u2013FR-5). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 7. Build `LoanPaymentSummary` as all latest personal loans left-joined to applied-payment aggregates, so loans with no payments get zero rows (FR-6\u2013FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups (summary by loan_id, payments by loan_id, loans of the JWT customer with summary) as table functions/relationships reading claims. [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n- [ ] 9. Review the finished pipeline for data-quality and observability needs and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded. [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n- [ ] 10. Write API snapshot tests for the acceptance criteria, including the returned payment, zero-payment loan, late fees, multi-loan customer, and the JWT authorization cases. [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 11. Create `run-tests.sh` with the declared environments and sub-project and run the suites. [deps: 10] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 12. Update the README with the project description, sources, API, authorization, and any discrepancy (esp. FR-8 coverage). [deps: 11] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n- [ ] 13. [cond: tests fail after step 11] Diagnose and fix failing tests. [deps: 11] \u2192 skill: /troubleshoot-test\n\nHmm, rule 9: create/update run-tests.sh step. Rule: place final testing and docs after DQ step. My order: DQ(9), tests(10), run-tests(11), docs(12). Good.\n\nWait, dependency of step 9 on 8 (API) is right. But step 8 depends on 4 (schema) and 7. Good.\n\nAlso need to make sure the run-tests step \"runs it\". Yes.\n\nShould there be a step to verify FR-8 coverage? I can fold into step 10 note (not testable; documented in README). I'll mention in step 10 or in the requirements source. Let me put a note in step 10: FR-8 is not exercised by catalog data; verify it by construction and document.\n\nNow Data Quality & Observability section:\n- Build: None. Explain the only silent-drop risk (personal payment with no matching loan) is guaranteed away by the catalog's `LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test` in `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl`.\n- Skip: Don't replicate the catalog's enum/referential assertions; don't count loan_type exclusions (intentional auto filter); no per-source ingestion counts because no independent senders.\n- Reuse: catalog lending ontology assertions and root ontology snapshot queries; sibling `lending/` has no DQ. \n\nHmm, but the rule wants \"state each Build decision as a risk, not a table.\" Since Build is empty, I'll say so. Actually, is there any credible failure? Consider FR-8: CDC status changes. If a payment version arrives with same `source_updated_at` but different status, DISTINCT might pick arbitrarily. Not a DQ thing. Consider `loan_purpose` nullable \u2014 API shows null. Fine.\n\nOne possible DQ: a payment referencing a loan whose latest version changed `primary_borrower_customer_id` \u2014 not an error. Fine.\n\nI'll set Build empty, but note in the checklist step 9 that it inspects and finds nothing new. Good.\n\n### Technical Decisions\nBullets as above.\n\n### Assumptions\nList with impact tags.\n\nNow, do I need to mention the exact include namespace? The task context says prefer underscores: `data_catalog`. Lending uses dash. I'll go with `data_catalog` and import `IMPORT data_catalog.lending.cards_consumer_credit.consumer_loans-{{environment}} AS cons_loans;`. And read-only module root path `../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json`.\n\nWait \u2014 but the task-context example uses package `../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json`. I'll state the include uses a top-level catalog package file and pin `is_batch:false`; the consumer environment selects the connector. To precisely follow lending (hard constraint) I'll say `../data-catalog/ontology-test-package.json` like `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`. Hmm, but the task-context says `ontology-shared-package.json`. Which is more likely to compile? Both are top-level. The difference: ontology-test-package.json sets script.config.environment=test at the include level. The consumer's own `{{environment}}` is unaffected. Both should work for the consumer's imported connector file. However, if the compiler resolves `{{environment}}` in catalog's ontology.sqrl during loading, ontology-test-package.json saves us. So `ontology-test-package.json` is safer and matches lending. I'll use that. But the task-context explicitly gives `ontology-shared-package.json`... \n\nLet me think: the task-context is generated for this project and says: \"Available shared module(s) ... `data-catalog` \u2014 namespace `data_catalog`, declare `\"data_catalog\": { \"package\": \"../data-catalog/ontology-shared-package.json\" }`\". And \"If a module's OWN scripts contain `{{...}}` placeholders (inspect them before importing), supply those values in that module's `script.include` entry via a `config` object\". The catalog DOES contain placeholders. So per task-context, use ontology-shared-package.json + supply environment in config. But then environment is pinned in the include config. If we put it in shared base, it's test forever. Unless overlays override `script.include.data_catalog.config.environment`. \n\nGiven the hard constraint says \"the same way as lending\", and lending pins `ontology-test-package.json` + `is_batch:false`, I'll follow lending: include the catalog's test package file at the include level (which supplies environment=test for the included module's own scripts) and `is_batch:false`; the consumer's `script.config.environment` chooses which connector file the consumer imports. I'll explicitly record this in Technical Decisions and Assumptions, noting the included module's internal environment is inert because only the consumer-selected connector is imported.\n\nHmm wait, is it true the included module's environment is inert? The consumer imports `consumer_loans-{{environment}}` (its own env). That file `IMPORT consumer_loans.*;` and defines tables. No other catalog file is imported. So yes.\n\nActually, hold on. There's a subtlety: DataSQRL may auto-include the `script.main` of the included package? No, script.include just makes files available. The include's `script.main` (ontology.sqrl) is not run. Only the consumer's main runs. Good.\n\nOK. Also note: The task-context says name include namespace with underscores. I'll use `data_catalog`.\n\nNow, one more consideration: custom schema vs inferred. If we use a custom schema, table tests (`/*+test*/`) won't run (use-inferred-schema false). The DQ/observability tables need their own `/*+test*/` snapshots per monitor-pipeline skill. But if we have a custom schema and no table tests run, DQ table tests can't use `/*+test*/` unless a second table-test package. Since we decided there are no DQ/OB tables, this is moot. But the API tests provide coverage. However, monitor-pipeline skill says \"Give every DQ table and every observability statistics table its own /*+test*/ snapshot.\" No DQ tables \u2192 none.\n\nBut wait: the acceptance criteria mention verifying counts like \"LoanPayments has 1,010 rows\". With a custom schema and API tests only, we can't directly count via `/*+test*/`. We can test specific queries (PL-115, CUST-060) as API snapshots. The requirement says \"Snapshot tests store the API responses above under the test folder of the project.\" So API tests suffice. Good. But the acceptance criterion \"LoanPayments has 1,010 rows\" \u2014 an API test with a limit/offset won't show the total. Hmm. Could add table tests in a second table-test package (`loan-payments-table-test-package.json`) for the count assertions (FR-1: 1010 rows) and FR-8-ish. The test-sqrl skill offers this exception: \"The requirements or the plan ask for a test that must not be in the API, for example a data integrity check or a validation query.\" The acceptance criteria FR-1 row count is a data integrity check not exposed via API. So I should include table tests in a separate table-test package.\n\nGiven the requirements list precise counts (1,010 rows, no AL- loan_id, one row per payment_id, one row per loan_id), these are data integrity checks. I'll plan a second test package for table tests. That adds complexity but aligns with skill. The plan should include:\n- API tests (main test package, use-inferred-schema false) for the lookups and auth.\n- Table tests (second package, use-inferred-schema true) for counts/uniqueness/filters (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3).\n\nAnd run-tests.sh must declare both sub-projects or suites. Hmm, the run-tests template uses sub-project names; a second test package for the same sub-project is a separate suite. The skill says rename to `<sub-project>-api-test-package.json` and create `<sub-project>-table-test-package.json`, and add `<sub-project>_table_test` to SUBPROJECTS and suite lines. That's the library_loans pattern.\n\nSo SUBPROJECTS would be `loan_payments loan_payments_table_test`. ENVS test/prod. But table-test only has a test config (table tests only run in test). The skill step 4 says table tests need test data. For the table test suite, ENVS still test/prod declared? That complicates. Given time, maybe simpler: include both API tests and table tests? The skill says keep them separate because each test package uses one schema.\n\nLet me decide: This is a plan for a downstream agent. I'll specify the two-package test structure, referencing the skill. The details are the agent's. I'll note in the checklist step for tests. The run-tests step will declare both suites. Actually the skill says only if the project has a mutation table step 5. We have no mutation. So table tests read same test data. Fine.\n\nHmm, but is it over-engineering? The requirements explicitly demand the API snapshot tests and mention the counts as acceptance criteria. The implementation agent can satisfy counts via table tests. I'll include it.\n\nLet me refine the checklist test steps:\n\n- Step 10: Add table tests (in an inferred-schema table-test package) for the data-integrity acceptance criteria: personal-only row count and no `AL-` loans, one row per id in each table. \u2192 /test-sqrl\n- Step 11: Add API tests (in the custom-schema package) for the lookups and JWT authorization acceptance criteria, including returned payments, zero-payment loans, and multi-loan customers. \u2192 /test-sqrl, /design-api\n- Step 12: run-tests.sh declares both suites and runs them. \u2192 /test-sqrl\n\nHmm but rule says run-tests step. And final docs after.\n\nActually, to reduce rist of over-specifying, I'll combine table+API tests into one step? The section rule says one or two sentences, can reference. But skill wants separate packages. I'll do two steps (table tests, API tests) then run-tests.\n\nNow, regarding FR-8: \"The plan says how it covers this rule.\" I'll address in step 11/table tests: no catalog payment changes status, so FR-8 is verified by construction (dedup latest then unbounded re-aggregate) and documented in README; optionally a table test is impossible without synthetic data (out of scope). I'll put this in the Data Quality/Technical Decisions and step note.\n\nLet me now also confirm the customer id mapping for PL-115 = CUST-439 and PL-011 = CUST-066, CUST-060 loans. Let me quickly grep the personal loan test data for those to make sure the plan's references are accurate. But the requirements already state CUST-439 for PL-115 and CUST-060 has PL-006,007,022,033. I'll trust. Maybe verify quickly.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning_content"}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "call_01a20cbb85657722b98e31ae", "name": "bash", "arguments": {"command": "cd /workspace/data-catalog/lending/cards_consumer_credit/testdata && grep -o '\"loan_id\":\"PL-115\"[^}]*' consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl | grep -o '\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"[^\"]*\"'; echo \"---CUST-060 loans---\"; grep -o '\"loan_id\":\"PL-[0-9]*\",\"application_id\":\"[^\"]*\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-060\"' consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl; echo \"---PL-011 cust---\"; grep -o '\"loan_id\":\"PL-011\"[^}]*' consumer_loans-personal_loan.jsonl | grep -o '\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"[^\"]*\"'; echo \"---personal payments count---\"; grep -c '\"loan_type\":\"PERSONAL\"' consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl; echo \"---applied personal---\"; grep '\"loan_type\":\"PERSONAL\"' consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl | grep -c '\"payment_status\":\"APPLIED\"'; echo \"---returned personal---\"; grep '\"loan_type\":\"PERSONAL\"' consumer_loans-loan_payment.jsonl | grep -c '\"payment_status\":\"RETURNED\"'"}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "together", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash", "usage": {"input": 415, "output": 5950, "cacheRead": 97920, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 5631, "totalTokens": 104285, "cost": {"input": 0.0001245, "output": 0.00714, "cacheRead": 0.00058752, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.00785202}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1795509932694, "responseId": "7ca92afcb05a4f0aa1a8b31c8db1aaaf-aws_ec1", "rawStopReason": "tool_calls", "durationMs": 13485, "thinkingLevel": "high"}, "toolResults": [{"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_01a20cbb85657722b98e31ae", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-439\"\n---CUST-060 loans---\n\"loan_id\":\"PL-006\",\"application_id\":\"LAPP-009\",\"primary_borrower_customer_id\":\"CUST-060\"\n\"loan_id\":\"PL-007\",\