{"request_uuid": "ce794cda-7704-4462-a744-ff62d48b00d8", "timestamp": "2026-11-24T08:46:02.121286+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "mode": "planning", "workspace_folder": "loan-payments", "mac_id": "5c:9b:a6:82:ff:92", "type": "message_update", "usage": {"input": 661, "output": 4016, "cacheRead": 106752, "cacheWrite": 0, "reasoning": 3713, "totalTokens": 111429, "cost": {"input": 0.0001983, "output": 0.0048192, "cacheRead": 0.000640512, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0.0056580120000000005}}, "assistantMessageEvent": {"type": "thinking_end", "contentIndex": 0, "content": "In library_loans, the table-test uses environment `table_test` which maps to `connectors/sources_table_test.sqrl` (local file, no mutations). For our project, we can set the table-test environment to `test` (using catalog test connectors) since no mutations. That's valid. But then the table test package would use the same connector as API test. That's fine.\n\nActually, wait: the API test package's custom schema with `use-inferred-schema: false` means `/*+test*/` table tests don't run. The table test package with `use-inferred-schema: true` and `environment: test` reads the same catalog test data. Fine.\n\nHowever, there's a concern: the table-test package will still compile the whole main script including the custom schema? No schema is configured in shared base. The shared base config includes `script.api.v1.schema`. Hmm, library_loans shared base has `script.api.v1.schema`. The table-test package layers shared base + table-test, so the custom schema is configured but `use-inferred-schema: true` makes the test server use the inferred schema. Good. The compile still validates the custom schema (which is good).\n\nOK. Now I have all I need. Let me write the plan.\n\nOne more: The main script will be named `loan_payments.sqrl` or `loan-payments.sqrl`. Names can have dashes? File names can. Sibling uses `lending360.sqrl`. I'll suggest `loan_payments.sqrl` (underscore) to keep sub-project names single-word with underscores. Actually sub-project name for build folder could be `loan_payments`. Fine.\n\nLet me now decide the exact checklist step count and content. I'll write ~13 steps.\n\nLet me refine with skills:\n\n1. Inspect catalog test data. [deps: none] \u2192 /data-observation\n2. Scaffold project from `lending/` layout. [deps: none] \u2192 /init-sqrl\n3. Write base + test/prod overlays with catalog include, engines, API version, test-runner. [deps: 2] \u2192 /configure-sqrl\n4. Define custom GraphQL schema + operations. [deps: 2] \u2192 /from-api, /design-api\n5. Configure vertx auth in overlays. [deps: 3] \u2192 /configure-sqrl\n6. Implement `LoanPayments` pipeline (dedup, filter, join). [deps: 3] \u2192 /implement-sqrl\n7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` (all loans left join aggregates). [deps: 6] \u2192 /implement-sqrl\n8. Implement the three auth-filtered lookups. [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n9. DQ/observability review. [deps: 8] \u2192 /monitor-pipeline\n10. Table tests for data-integrity acceptance criteria in a separate inferred-schema test package. [deps: 8] \u2192 /test-sqrl\n11. API tests for lookups + JWT auth in the custom-schema test package. [deps: 9, 10]? Actually API tests depend on schema (4) and pipeline (8), not on table tests. Let me set deps 8. And table tests deps 8. They're independent; list separately.\n12. run-tests.sh declares both suites and runs. [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 /test-sqrl\n13. README/docs. [deps: 12] \u2192 /write-documentation\n14. [cond: tests fail after step 12] troubleshoot. [deps: 12] \u2192 /troubleshoot-test\n\nWait, rule 11 says DQ step after all pipeline tasks including API. I put DQ (9) after 8. Tests (10,11) after 9. Good. Docs after.\n\nNow, step 10 table tests \u2014 should it depend on DQ step 9? \"Place final testing and documentation after this step so they include the monitoring.\" Yes, tests after DQ. So deps 9 for both test steps.\n\nLet me set:\n10. table tests [deps: 9]\n11. API tests [deps: 9]\n12. run-tests [deps: 10, 11]\n13. docs [deps: 12]\n14. cond troubleshoot [deps: 12]\n\nAlso should I include a step to remove the FR-8 note? No.\n\nNow I realize step 9 DQ perhaps should produce a \"nothing new\" conclusion. Fine.\n\nAlso, the plan's checklist rule 10 says include a shared-base-config step; step 3 covers that.\n\nNow let me write the file.\n\nFor frontmatter:\n```\n---\ndomains:\n  - finance\nscenario: pipeline\n---\n\n# Implementation Plan: Personal Loan Payments API\n\n**Scenario**: pipeline\n**Task**: new-project\n**Date**: 2026-11-24T08:44:55Z\n**Status**: DRAFT\n```\n\nThen a sentence.\n\n## Requirements Source\n`adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`; sibling layout `lending/`; catalog shared module `data-catalog`.\n\n## Implementation Checklist\nwith the legend line. The example legend: \n\"Legend: `- [ ]` = not started, `- [*]` = in progress, `- [x]` = passing, `- [~]` = blocked/skipped\" \u2014 the template didn't specify exact legend text but said \"Write the 4-state legend line (shown in the ADR Format template) directly under the heading\". The ADR format template didn't actually show a legend line; the checklist rules mention 4-state legend. I'll write a sensible legend line.\n\nNow write.\n\nFor the Implementation Checklist, I must not include fenced code blocks. Plain checkbox lines.\n\nLet me be careful about referencing external paths. I'll reference `adr/requirements_20261124-113440.md`, project files to be created (paths), and the catalog by module name. I need to give full file paths matching layout. Since this is a pipeline (not catalog), the catalog layout rule doesn't apply. Good.\n\nLet me draft each section now.\n\nChecklist steps text (concise, 1-2 sentences, cite requirements):\n\n- [ ] 1. Inspect the catalog consumer-loan test data to confirm the personal-loan and loan-payment payload shapes and the edge cases (`PL-115`, `PL-011`, auto-loan rows) the acceptance criteria rely on. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /data-observation\n- [ ] 2. Scaffold the project beside the sibling `lending/` with a main script, an API folder, a snapshots folder, and a README. [deps: none] \u2192 skill: /init-sqrl\n- [ ] 3. Write `loan-payments-shared-package.json` plus the `test` and `prod` overlays, declaring the catalog under `script.include`, the Flink/Postgres/Vert.x engine set, the versioned API, and the test runner (FR-1; Open Questions). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 4. Define the custom GraphQL schema and operations for the three lookups, keeping the exact table/type names and leaving `customer_id` out of the caller-visible arguments (FR-9, FR-10, FR-11; see `### API surface`). [deps: 2] \u2192 skill: /from-api, /design-api\n- [ ] 5. Configure the Vert.x auth provider in the `test` and `prod` overlays so the claim-reading functions compile (FR-12, FR-13). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /configure-sqrl\n- [ ] 6. Implement the `LoanPayments` table: import the catalog consumer-loan scripts, keep the latest version of each loan and payment by `source_updated_at`, retain only `loan_type = 'PERSONAL'` payments, and inner-join them to their loan for the customer and loan fields (FR-1\u2013FR-5, `### Transformations and aggregations` items 1\u20133). [deps: 3] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 7. Implement `LoanPaymentSummary` across all latest personal loans, aggregating only `APPLIED` payments and left-joining so a loan with no payments gets zero counts and a null last payment date (FR-6, FR-7, FR-8). [deps: 6] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl\n- [ ] 8. Add the three authorization-filtered lookups by `loan_id` and by the JWT `customer_id` claim, including the summary relationship of the customer-loans query (FR-9\u2013FR-12). [deps: 4, 7] \u2192 skill: /implement-sqrl, /design-api\n- [ ] 9. Review the completed pipeline for data-quality and observability gaps and add tables only where a credible failure is unguarded; otherwise record why nothing new is needed (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 8] \u2192 skill: /monitor-pipeline\n- [ ] 10. Add a separate inferred-schema test package with table tests for the data-integrity criteria: personal-only row count, absence of `AL-` loans, and one row per `payment_id` and per `loan_id` (FR-1, FR-2, FR-3). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 11. Add API tests in the custom-schema test package for the snapshot criteria and the JWT cases: the returned payment, a loan with late fees, the zero-payment loan, the four-loan customer, a wrong customer, and a missing token (acceptance criteria FR-5\u2013FR-13). [deps: 9] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 12. Create `run-tests.sh` declaring the `test`/`prod` environments and both test suites, then run it and regenerate snapshots (FR-2 test command). [deps: 10, 11] \u2192 skill: /test-sqrl\n- [ ] 13. Document the project, its sources, API, authorization, and the FR-8 coverage decision in the README (see `## Data Quality & Observability`). [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /write-documentation\n- [ ] 14. [cond: tests fail after step 12] Diagnose and fix the failing compile or snapshot. [deps: 12] \u2192 skill: /troubleshoot-test\n\nHmm step 10/11 both deps 9; they're independent, list separately. Good.\n\nBut the test-sqrl skill says API tests should be the ones that check data the API returns; table tests for data that must not be in the API. The acceptance counts (row counts) are data-integrity checks. But FR-1's \"1010 rows\" is arguably an API-relevant check. It's fine as table test. Actually, is a row-count table test storing a snapshot of 1010 rows? We'd do `CountTest := SELECT COUNT(*) ...`. That's a small snapshot. Good. The requirement says \"Snapshot tests store the API responses above under the test folder.\" So API tests must cover the listed ones; table tests add the counts. Fine.\n\nWait, but requirement acceptance: \"(FR-1, FR-3) LoanPayments has 1,010 rows. No row has a loan_id that starts with AL-.\" These are not API responses per se. Table test fine.\n\nAlso FR-2: one row per payment_id / loan_id. Table test with GROUP BY HAVING count>1 (no_rows). Good.\n\nNow DQ section:\n- Build: None new. State the only silent-drop risk and why the catalog guarantees it: the inner join from personal payments to loans can only lose rows if a `PERSONAL` payment has no `Personal_Loan`; the catalog asserts that away in `data-catalog/lending/lending-ontology.sqrl` (`LoanPaymentReferencesValidPersonalLoan_Test`), so no capture. Also note FR-8 status-change re-aggregation is by design.\n- Skip: Don't repeat the catalog's enum, referential-integrity or numeric assertions; don't emit ingestion counters for the two sources because their independent-sender failure mode does not apply and root snapshot queries already flag content changes; don't monitor the intentional auto-loan exclusion.\n- Reuse: catalog assertions and root ontology snapshots; sibling `lending/` carries no DQ either.\n\nHmm \"Build: none\" but the rule says each Build decision as risk. Since none, state \"None.\"\n\nTechnical Decisions:\n- Custom GraphQL schema + operations (not inferred) because customer_id must be a JWT-injected claim and the type names are fixed; schema validation and REST/MCP bounds follow `lending360-api/`.\n- Engines Flink + Postgres + Vert.x; no kafka engine because the API has no mutations or subscriptions, and catalog Kafka sources are external Flink connectors.\n- Catalog included under `script.include` with a top-level catalog package file and `is_batch: false`; the consumer's per-environment `script.config.environment` selects the `-test`/`-prod` connector file, matching `lending/lending360-shared-package.json`.\n- CDC dedup via `DISTINCT ... ON <key> ORDER BY source_updated_at DESC`, and an unwindowed aggregation so late or changed payments update the summary within the streaming freshness target.\n- `LoanPaymentSummary` is anchored on the latest personal loans and left-joins applied-payment aggregates, so loans with no payments still produce a row.\n- Auth: claim-injected table functions/relationships plus a Vert.x `auth` issuer/key block (HS256 fixed secret in test, `${JWT_SECRET}` in prod); a NOT NULL claim argument yields the required authorization error.\n\nAssumptions:\n- FR-8 coverage: Assumed FR-8 is verified by construction (latest-version dedup feeding an unwindowed re-aggregation) rather than by a snapshot, because no catalog payment changes status. Rationale: the catalog test data has no PENDING/REVERSED and no status transitions, and adding synthetic source data is out of scope. [impact: low]\n- Include config: Assumed the catalog is included through a top-level catalog package file pinned to the test environment with `is_batch: false`, exactly like `lending/`, while the consumer's `environment` picks the connector. Rationale: the consumer imports only the `-{{environment}}` connector file; the include's own environment is inert. [impact: medium]\n- Dev environment: Assumed only `test` and `prod` overlays, as required. [impact: low]\n- etc.\n\nLet me include the Open Question assumptions:\n- Freshness: within 1 minute via streaming Flink, no window. [high]\n- JWT: HS256 test secret; `${JWT_SECRET}` prod; `${AUTH_ISSUER}`/`${AUTH_AUDIENCE}` placeholders. [high]\n- Production workload: up to 1M loans, 50k payments/day, 50 rps peak; smallest Cloud deployment. [medium]\n- last_payment_date: latest applied_date of applied payments. [low]\n- total_paid_cents: sum payment_amount_cents of applied. [low]\n- Loans without payments: included with zeros/null. [medium]\n- Loan status: every status. [medium]\n- Customer query loan details: add loan_status, original_amount_cents, current_balance_cents, monthly_payment_cents, next_payment_due_date. [low]\n- API protocols: GraphQL, REST, MCP. [low]\n- Budget: smallest DataSQRL Cloud deployment. [low]\n\nThat's a lot but fine.\n\nNow write. Let me create the skeleton first with Write, then Edits.\n\nActually the instructions: create skeleton with every heading followed by `[PENDING: <section name>]`, then Edit per section. Let me do that.\n\nSkeleton content:\n\n```\n---\ndomains:\n  - finance\nscenario: pipeline\n---\n\n# Implementation Plan: Personal Loan Payments API\n\n**Scenario**: pipeline\n**Task**: new-project\n**Date**: 2026-11-24T08:44:55Z\n**Status**: DRAFT\n\nA new DataSQRL pipeline in `loan-payments/` that ... reuses the shared `data-catalog`...\n\n---\n\n## Requirements Source\n[PENDING: Requirements Source]\n\n## Implementation Checklist\n[PENDING: Implementation Checklist]\n\n## Data Quality & Observability\n[PENDING: Data Quality & Observability]\n\n## Technical Decisions\n[PENDING: Technical Decisions]\n\n## Assumptions\n[PENDING: Assumptions]\n```\n\nThen edits.\n\nThe one/two sentence summary goes after the header lines before the `---`.\n\nLet me write it."}}
